window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"NE-1":{"unit":"NE-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01","title":"Neural Tube Embryology and Its Malformations","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the central nervous system from a flat sheet of ectoderm to a closed tube, then to the vesicles that become the adult brain and ventricles, and to the three tissues that supply its cells. Each developmental step is paired with the malformation that follows when it fails: open neural tube defects when the neuropores do not close, holoprosencephaly when the forebrain does not divide, and the Chiari and Dandy-Walker malformations when the hindbrain and posterior fossa develop abnormally. Board questions give a screening result, a facial or skin finding, or an imaging description and ask you to name the step that failed. Timing, signals, and tissue of origin let you reason from finding to mechanism.","prerequisites":["Gastrulation, the trilaminar germ disc, and formation of the notochord (week 3)","Gross organization of the brain: hemispheres, diencephalon, brainstem, cerebellum, and ventricles","Folate and one-carbon metabolism in thymidylate and purine synthesis","Meiotic nondisjunction and the autosomal trisomies","Basic signal transduction: ligands, receptors, and transcription factors"],"connections":[{"to":"Neuron and nerve histology (NE-2)","how":"The glial cells whose origins are set here (oligodendrocytes, astrocytes, Schwann cells, microglia) respond differently to injury because they come from different tissues."},{"to":"CNS blood supply and CSF flow (NE-10)","how":"The ventricles are the persistent lumen of the neural tube, so the CSF pathway follows the vesicles and explains where hydrocephalus begins."},{"to":"Diseases of increased intracranial fluid (NE-20-C)","how":"Aqueductal stenosis, Chiari II, and Dandy-Walker malformation are developmental causes of obstructive hydrocephalus."},{"to":"Neurogenetics (NE-16)","how":"Trisomy 13 and single-gene defects in the sonic hedgehog pathway are major causes of holoprosencephaly."},{"to":"Congenital gastrointestinal disease (GI-13-H)","how":"Hirschsprung disease is a failure of neural crest cells to colonize the distal colon, a direct consequence of crest migration described here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Neurulation","def":"Formation of the neural tube from the neural plate during weeks 3 and 4."},{"term":"Notochord","def":"A rod of axial mesoderm that induces the neural plate and later persists only as the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs."},{"term":"Neural plate","def":"The thickened strip of dorsal ectoderm (neuroectoderm) that forms over the notochord and folds into the neural tube."},{"term":"Neural fold","def":"The raised lateral edge of the neural plate; paired folds meet and fuse in the midline to close the tube."},{"term":"Neural crest","def":"Cells that detach from the crests of the neural folds during fusion and migrate to form peripheral neurons, glia, and many non-neural tissues."},{"term":"Neuropore","def":"One of the two open ends of the neural tube, cranial and caudal, which are the last parts of the tube to close."},{"term":"Brain vesicle","def":"A dilation of the cranial neural tube; three primary vesicles divide into five secondary vesicles that form the adult brain."},{"term":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)","def":"The main fetal plasma protein, made by the yolk sac and fetal liver, which leaks into amniotic fluid and maternal blood from open neural tube defects."},{"term":"Sonic hedgehog (SHH)","def":"A secreted, cholesterol-modified signaling protein that specifies ventral midline tissue in the neural tube, forebrain, and face."},{"term":"Holoprosencephaly","def":"Failure of the prosencephalon to divide into two cerebral hemispheres, with accompanying midline facial defects."},{"term":"Syringomyelia","def":"A fluid-filled cavity (syrinx) within the spinal cord, most often cervical and often associated with Chiari I malformation."},{"term":"Posterior fossa","def":"The compartment of the cranial cavity below the tentorium that holds the cerebellum, pons, medulla, and fourth ventricle and opens at the foramen magnum."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C01","obj":"NE-1.1","title":"Neurulation: induction, folding, fusion, and neuropore closure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neurulation is the process by which ectoderm on the dorsal surface of the embryo becomes the neural tube, the precursor of the brain and spinal cord. It begins in week 3, while gastrulation is still adding mesoderm at the caudal end of the embryo, and the tube is closed by the end of week 4."},{"type":"p","text":"The notochord, a rod of axial mesoderm formed during gastrulation, and the prechordal mesoderm in front of it induce the overlying ectoderm to become neural. Ectoderm exposed to bone morphogenetic protein 4 (BMP4) becomes epidermis. The notochord and prechordal mesoderm secrete the BMP antagonists noggin, chordin, and follistatin, which bind BMP4 and keep it from its receptors, and ectoderm released from BMP signaling adopts its default neural fate. The result, at about day 18 to 19, is the neural plate: a thickened strip of pseudostratified neuroectoderm lying directly over the notochord and broader at its cranial end."},{"type":"p","text":"The lateral edges of the plate rise as neural folds, leaving the neural groove in the midline between them. Elevation is driven partly by the plate cells themselves, which constrict their apical surfaces with actin and myosin and become wedge shaped at hinge points, and partly by expansion of the adjacent mesoderm and surface ectoderm. The folds first meet and fuse in the future cervical region, at about the level of the fifth somite, around day 22. Fusion then proceeds both cranially and caudally. As the folds fuse, surface ectoderm separates from the tube and closes over it as future epidermis, and cells at the crests of the folds detach as neural crest."},{"type":"flow","title":"Neurulation and neuropore closure","steps":["Notochord secretes noggin, chordin, and follistatin","BMP4 blocked; ectoderm forms neural plate (about day 18 to 19)","Plate edges rise as neural folds around the neural groove","Folds fuse first in future cervical region (about day 22)","Fusion spreads cranially and caudally; neural crest detaches","Cranial neuropore closes about day 25","Caudal neuropore closes about day 28"],"caption":"Fusion starts in the middle of the tube and spreads toward both ends, so the two neuropores are the last openings to seal and the commonest sites of failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Until fusion is complete, the two ends of the tube remain open to the amniotic cavity as the cranial (anterior) and caudal (posterior) neuropores. The cranial neuropore closes at about day 25 and the caudal neuropore at about day 28. As the last sites to close, they are the commonest sites of failure: failed cranial closure produces anencephaly, and failed caudal closure produces lumbosacral myelomeningocele (C04). Once both close, the lumen is a sealed cavity that becomes the ventricles and the central canal. The lowest sacral and coccygeal cord segments form afterward by secondary neurulation, in which a solid mass of tail bud cells hollows out and joins the primary tube."},{"type":"p","text":"The closed tube is then patterned dorsoventrally. Sonic hedgehog (SHH) from the notochord and from the floor plate of the tube directs ventral cells to become motor neurons (basal plate), while BMPs from the roof plate and overlying ectoderm specify dorsal sensory neurons (alar plate). This is why motor nuclei lie ventral, or medial in the brainstem, and sensory nuclei lie dorsal, or lateral."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A teratogen acts only on day 32 of development. Could it cause anencephaly or an open myelomeningocele?","explain":"No. Both neuropores have closed by about day 28, and an open neural tube defect requires interference with closure itself. An insult after closure can damage the formed brain or cord but cannot reopen the tube.","check":"Compare with the neuropore closure days in the flow and the paragraph that follows it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C02","obj":"NE-1.2","title":"Brain vesicles, their adult derivatives, and the ventricles","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"By the end of week 4, as the cranial neuropore closes, the cranial end of the tube expands into three primary vesicles: the prosencephalon (forebrain), mesencephalon (midbrain), and rhombencephalon (hindbrain). The caudal tube stays narrow and becomes the spinal cord. The tube also bends at three flexures: the cephalic flexure at the midbrain and the cervical flexure at the hindbrain and cord junction, both concave ventrally, and the pontine flexure between them, which bends the opposite way and spreads the thin roof of the hindbrain."},{"type":"p","text":"In week 5 two of the primary vesicles divide, giving five secondary vesicles. The prosencephalon divides into the telencephalon, whose paired lateral outgrowths become the cerebral hemispheres, and the diencephalon. The rhombencephalon divides into the metencephalon and myelencephalon. The mesencephalon does not divide. Each secondary vesicle produces a fixed set of adult structures."},{"type":"table","title":"Brain vesicles and their adult derivatives","cols":["Primary vesicle","Secondary vesicle","Adult structures","Cavity"],"rows":[["Prosencephalon","Telencephalon","Cerebral cortex and white matter, caudate and putamen, hippocampus, amygdala, olfactory bulbs","Lateral ventricles"],["Prosencephalon","Diencephalon","Thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus with pineal gland, retina and optic nerve, posterior pituitary","Third ventricle"],["Mesencephalon","Mesencephalon","Midbrain: tectum (colliculi), tegmentum, cerebral peduncles","Cerebral aqueduct"],["Rhombencephalon","Metencephalon","Pons and cerebellum","Upper fourth ventricle"],["Rhombencephalon","Myelencephalon","Medulla oblongata","Lower fourth ventricle"],["Caudal neural tube","None","Spinal cord","Central canal"]],"caption":"The lumen of each vesicle persists as a ventricle, so the cavity identifies the vesicle of origin."},{"type":"p","text":"The lumen of the tube is never lost; it widens or narrows with the wall around it. The lateral ventricles are the cavities of the hemispheres and drain through the interventricular foramina (of Monro) into the third ventricle of the diencephalon. The walls of the mesencephalon thicken greatly, so its lumen narrows to the cerebral aqueduct, which joins the fourth ventricle of the rhombencephalon. The roof of the fourth ventricle, thinned by the pontine flexure, carries choroid plexus and later opens through the median and lateral apertures into the subarachnoid space. The aqueduct is the narrowest channel in the system, so aqueductal stenosis is a classic cause of congenital obstructive hydrocephalus."},{"type":"p","text":"Several structures that seem separate from the brain are outgrowths of the diencephalon. The optic vesicle forms the retina and optic nerve, so the optic nerve is a CNS tract myelinated by oligodendrocytes and can be affected by multiple sclerosis. The infundibulum grows down from the diencephalic floor to form the posterior pituitary, and the pineal gland arises from its roof. The anterior pituitary, by contrast, comes from Rathke pouch, an outgrowth of oral ectoderm, not from the neural tube."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing an obstruction by which ventricles are enlarged","text":"A fetal MRI shows enlarged lateral and third ventricles and a normal fourth ventricle. CSF is made mainly by the choroid plexus of the lateral ventricles and flows through the interventricular foramina to the third ventricle, through the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle, and out through its apertures. Every space upstream of a block dilates and every space downstream does not. The lateral and third ventricles are enlarged, so the block lies beyond the third ventricle; the fourth is normal, so the block lies before it. The only channel between them is the aqueduct, the lumen of the mesencephalon, so this is aqueductal stenosis. If the fourth ventricle were also greatly dilated, the obstruction would lie at its outlet, as in Dandy-Walker malformation (C06)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C03","obj":"NE-1.3","title":"Neuroectoderm, neural crest, and mesoderm derivatives","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The nervous system draws its cells from three sources, and the source of a cell predicts where it lives, how it behaves, and which diseases arise from it. Neuroectoderm is the wall of the neural tube itself. Neural crest is the population that detaches from the crests of the neural folds during fusion. Mesoderm contributes the microglia, the dura, and the blood vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuroepithelial cells lining the tube divide in the ventricular zone and produce, first, neuroblasts, which become all CNS neurons, and later glioblasts, which become astrocytes and oligodendrocytes. Radial glia act both as progenitors and as scaffolds along which young neurons migrate outward to form the cortex. Cells that remain lining the lumen become ependyma, the ciliated epithelium of the ventricles and central canal, and specialized ependyma forms the epithelium of the choroid plexus. The retina, the glia of the optic nerve, the posterior pituitary, and the pineal gland are also neuroectoderm because they grow out of the tube (C02)."},{"type":"p","text":"Neural crest cells undergo an epithelial to mesenchymal transition: they lose their E-cadherin junctions, become motile, and migrate along defined pathways. In the trunk they form the sensory neurons of the dorsal root ganglia, the postganglionic neurons of sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric ganglia, the Schwann cells and satellite cells of peripheral nerves and ganglia, the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, and melanocytes. In the head they also form much of the facial skeleton and connective tissue, sensory ganglia of several cranial nerves (with ectodermal placodes), odontoblasts, and, in cardiac crest, the aorticopulmonary septum. The adrenal medulla shows how origin explains function: chromaffin cells are modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons, so they receive preganglionic cholinergic fibers directly and release mainly epinephrine into the blood rather than onto a target organ."},{"type":"p","text":"Microglia are the exception among CNS glia. They derive from yolk sac macrophage progenitors (mesoderm) that colonize the brain early, so they belong to the mononuclear phagocyte lineage: they phagocytose debris, present antigen, and are the main CNS cells infected by HIV, fusing into multinucleated giant cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Cell origins in the nervous system","cols":["Origin","Derivatives","Disease examples"],"rows":[["Neuroectoderm (wall of the neural tube)","CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependyma, choroid plexus epithelium, retina, posterior pituitary, pineal gland","Multiple sclerosis (oligodendrocytes), astrocytoma, ependymoma, retinoblastoma"],["Neural crest","Dorsal root and cranial sensory ganglia, autonomic and enteric ganglia, Schwann and satellite cells, adrenal medulla, melanocytes, facial skeleton, odontoblasts, aorticopulmonary septum","Hirschsprung disease, neuroblastoma, pheochromocytoma, melanoma, schwannoma"],["Mesoderm","Microglia (yolk sac macrophage lineage), dura mater, CNS blood vessels","Multinucleated giant cells of HIV encephalitis (microglia)"],["Surface ectoderm (for contrast)","Anterior pituitary (Rathke pouch), lens, epidermis, inner ear and olfactory epithelium (placodes)","Craniopharyngioma (Rathke pouch remnant)"]],"caption":"Cells that stay inside the brain and cord come from the tube; cells scattered in the periphery come from crest; microglia come from mesoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"The two myelinating cells show why origin matters clinically. Oligodendrocytes (neuroectoderm) each myelinate segments of many CNS axons and are the target in multiple sclerosis. Schwann cells (neural crest) each myelinate a single internode of one peripheral axon, support axon regeneration, are the target in Guillain-Barre syndrome, and give rise to schwannomas. Because crest cells migrate, disorders of crest migration or proliferation appear far from the neural tube: Hirschsprung disease when crest fails to reach the distal colon, neuroblastoma and pheochromocytoma in the adrenal medulla, and melanoma in the skin."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has an adrenal tumor that secretes catecholamines. Does it share a tissue of origin with an ependymoma of the fourth ventricle?","explain":"No. Adrenal medullary tumors (neuroblastoma, pheochromocytoma) arise from neural crest cells that migrated away from the tube, whereas ependymoma arises from neuroectoderm that stayed lining the lumen.","check":"Compare with the neural crest and neuroectoderm rows of the cell origin table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C04","obj":"NE-1.4","title":"Neural tube defects, alpha-fetoprotein, and folate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neural tube defects result from failure of the tube to close, or from incomplete formation of its coverings. The closed tube induces the surrounding mesoderm to form the vertebral arches and skull vault, so an open tube leaves the bony covering incomplete. The defects are ranked by how much neural tissue is involved and whether skin covers it, which determines both the neurological outcome and the screening result."},{"type":"table","title":"Neural tube defects compared","cols":["Defect","What is in the defect","Skin covered","Amniotic AFP and AChE","Typical findings"],"rows":[["Spina bifida occulta","Unfused vertebral arch only, usually L5 or S1","Yes","Normal","Usually silent; hair tuft, dimple, or lipoma over the defect"],["Meningocele","Meninges and CSF; cord in place","Usually","Normal if skin covered; raised if open","Neurological function usually normal"],["Myelomeningocele","Meninges plus cord or roots, usually lumbosacral","Usually not","Raised","Deficits below the level, neurogenic bladder, Chiari II, hydrocephalus"],["Anencephaly","No skull vault; exposed, degenerated forebrain","No","Raised","Polyhydramnios; not compatible with survival"]],"caption":"Only defects exposing neural tissue to amniotic fluid raise AFP and AChE."},{"type":"p","text":"Myelomeningocele is the most common serious open defect and usually reflects failed closure of the caudal neuropore. The exposed cord and roots are further damaged by amniotic fluid, so function is lost below the level: lower motor neuron leg weakness, sensory loss, and neurogenic bladder and bowel from involvement of S2 to S4. Nearly every child with a lumbosacral myelomeningocele also has a Chiari II malformation, and most develop hydrocephalus (C06). Anencephaly is the cranial counterpart: the exposed forebrain degenerates, the skull vault does not form, and impaired fetal swallowing causes polyhydramnios."},{"type":"p","text":"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is the main fetal plasma protein, made by the yolk sac and then the fetal liver. Normally little reaches the amniotic fluid, mainly through fetal urine. In an open defect, fetal plasma leaks from exposed neural tissue and its vessels, raising amniotic AFP, and more AFP then crosses to the mother, raising maternal serum AFP (MSAFP), usually screened at about 15 to 20 weeks. Acetylcholinesterase (AChE), a neural enzyme not normally detectable in amniotic fluid, appears only when neural tissue is exposed, so it is more specific. Skin-covered lesions leave both values normal."},{"type":"flow","title":"From folate deficiency to raised maternal serum AFP","steps":["Low folate slows DNA synthesis in dividing neural folds","Caudal neuropore fails to close by about day 28","Vertebral arches cannot form over the open tube","Exposed neural tissue leaks AFP and acetylcholinesterase","Amniotic fluid AFP and acetylcholinesterase rise","AFP crosses to the mother, raising maternal serum AFP"],"caption":"Each step explains one finding."},{"type":"p","text":"Folate deficiency is the best established modifiable risk factor. Tetrahydrofolate carries one-carbon units: N5,N10-methylene tetrahydrofolate donates the carbon thymidylate synthase uses to make dTMP, and N10-formyl tetrahydrofolate supplies two carbons of the purine ring, so limited folate slows DNA synthesis in the rapidly dividing neural folds. Folate also remethylates homocysteine to methionine for DNA methylation, and raised maternal homocysteine and some MTHFR variants increase risk. Because the tube closes by day 28, the mother's folate status at conception is what counts, and periconceptional folic acid substantially lowers risk. Other risk factors are valproate (which interferes with folate metabolism and inhibits histone deacetylases), pregestational diabetes, obesity, and hyperthermia in early pregnancy."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting a raised maternal serum AFP","text":"An MSAFP is reported as 2.8 multiples of the median (MoM). AFP rises through the second trimester, so each result is compared with the median for that gestational age; about 2.0 to 2.5 MoM is the usual cutoff. First ask whether the value is truly high: a pregnancy further along than recorded makes a normal value look raised, and twins double the fetal source. With both confirmed, the remaining causes are leaks of fetal plasma: an open neural tube defect, gastroschisis or omphalocele, or fetal death. Raised amniotic acetylcholinesterase points to exposed neural tissue. A low MSAFP is instead associated with trisomy 21 and 18."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C05","obj":"NE-1.5","title":"Holoprosencephaly, sonic hedgehog, and the midline face","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Holoprosencephaly is failure of the prosencephalon to divide into two cerebral hemispheres with two lateral ventricles, a division that normally takes place at about weeks 5 to 6. It is a spectrum. In alobar holoprosencephaly there is a single midline ventricle, fused thalami, and no interhemispheric fissure or corpus callosum. In semilobar forms the posterior hemispheres are partly separated, and in lobar forms the hemispheres are largely separate, with fusion limited to the basal frontal region. Olfactory bulbs and tracts are often absent in severe forms."},{"type":"p","text":"The forebrain divides because its ventral midline is specified by signals from the prechordal plate, the mesoderm just cranial to the notochord. The key signal is sonic hedgehog (SHH). SHH binds its receptor Patched, which relieves Patched inhibition of Smoothened and activates GLI transcription factors that drive midline gene expression. The resulting midline tissue separates the single forebrain field into right and left halves. Loss of function anywhere along this pathway can produce holoprosencephaly: mutations in SHH itself or in other midline genes such as ZIC2 and SIX3, defective cholesterol synthesis, and teratogens. Cholesterol matters because SHH is covalently attached to cholesterol during its processing and Smoothened activity depends on sterols, so Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome (7-dehydrocholesterol reductase deficiency) can cause holoprosencephaly. Cyclopamine, a plant alkaloid that blocks Smoothened, produces cyclopia in grazing animals."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sonic hedgehog and division of the forebrain","steps":["Prechordal plate and ventral forebrain secrete cholesterol-modified SHH","SHH binds Patched, releasing Smoothened to activate GLI","Midline tissue forms in the ventral forebrain and midface","Single eye field splits into two eye primordia","Prosencephalon divides into two telencephalic hemispheres"],"caption":"Loss of any step (SHH, ZIC2, or SIX3 mutation, trisomy 13, defective cholesterol synthesis, or Smoothened blockade) leaves a single forebrain and a deficient midface."},{"type":"p","text":"Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome) is the most common chromosomal cause, and holoprosencephaly is one of its characteristic features alongside microphthalmia, cleft lip and palate, postaxial polydactyly, scalp defects (cutis aplasia), congenital heart disease, and severe intellectual disability. Holoprosencephaly also occurs with other chromosome abnormalities, including trisomy 18, and is more common in infants of mothers with pregestational diabetes."},{"type":"p","text":"The face is involved because the same midline signal shapes the midface. Early on, a single eye field lies across the anterior neural plate, and SHH from the prechordal plate suppresses its midline portion so that two eyes form. The nasal septum, philtrum, and premaxilla develop from the medial nasal prominences, which also depend on midline signaling. With too little midline tissue the eyes lie close together (hypotelorism) or fuse into a single median eye (cyclopia, often with a proboscis above it), the nose may have a single nostril, the premaxilla is absent, giving a median cleft lip, and a single central maxillary incisor may be the only sign of the mildest form. Because brain and face share one signal, the severity of the facial defect usually parallels the severity of the brain defect. The common lateral cleft lip is different: it reflects failed fusion of the maxillary prominence with the merged medial nasal prominences and does not by itself imply holoprosencephaly."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-01-01-C06","obj":"NE-1.6","title":"Chiari I, Chiari II, and Dandy-Walker malformations","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The three posterior fossa malformations are defined by where the cerebellum lies relative to the foramen magnum, by fossa size, and by the effect on CSF flow."},{"type":"table","title":"Posterior fossa malformations compared","cols":["Feature","Chiari I","Chiari II","Dandy-Walker"],"rows":[["Posterior fossa","Small","Small","Enlarged"],["Displaced or missing tissue","Tonsils at least about 5 mm below the foramen magnum","Vermis, fourth ventricle, and medulla","Vermis absent or hypoplastic; no herniation"],["Fourth ventricle","Normal position","Elongated, small, displaced downward","Dilated into a cyst"],["Neural tube defect","No","Lumbosacral myelomeningocele, almost always","No"],["Hydrocephalus","Uncommon","Common","Common"],["Usual presentation","Adolescence or adulthood; cough headache","Birth; open spine, stridor, apnea","Infancy; enlarging head"]],"caption":"Fossa size separates Dandy-Walker from both Chiari forms; the open spine and extra displaced structures separate Chiari II from Chiari I."},{"type":"p","text":"In Chiari I malformation the cerebellar tonsils extend at least about 5 mm below the foramen magnum, usually because the posterior fossa is too small for its contents (underdeveloped occipital bone, a paraxial mesoderm derivative). The brainstem and fourth ventricle stay in place, and there is no neural tube defect. It is often incidental or causes occipital headache worsened by coughing or straining, which raises intracranial pressure and pushes the tonsils down. Crowding at the foramen magnum impairs the normal pulsatile movement of CSF between cranial and spinal subarachnoid spaces, and the altered pressure waves are thought to drive fluid into the cord, forming a cervical syrinx (syringomyelia) in a substantial proportion of patients."},{"type":"p","text":"A cervical syrinx expands from the center of the cord. It first interrupts the pain and temperature fibers crossing in the anterior white commissure, giving bilateral loss of pain and temperature in a cape over the shoulders and arms while the dorsal columns, and therefore fine touch, vibration, and proprioception, are spared (dissociated sensory loss). As it enlarges it destroys anterior horn cells, causing lower motor neuron weakness and wasting of the hand muscles, and compresses the lateral corticospinal tracts, causing upper motor neuron signs in the legs."},{"type":"p","text":"Chiari II malformation is congenital and more severe. The posterior fossa is small, and the vermis, fourth ventricle, and medulla are displaced through the foramen magnum, with kinking of the medulla and beaking of the tectum. It occurs almost only with an open lumbosacral myelomeningocele, and the leading hypothesis links the two (flow below). Displaced tissue blocks outflow from the fourth ventricle, and aqueductal stenosis is common, causing obstructive hydrocephalus. Stretch on the brainstem and lower cranial nerves explains neonatal stridor from vocal cord paresis, swallowing difficulty, and apnea."},{"type":"flow","title":"Leading hypothesis for Chiari II malformation","steps":["Caudal neuropore fails: open lumbosacral myelomeningocele","CSF escapes through the open spinal defect","Ventricles stay undistended; posterior fossa fails to enlarge","Growing hindbrain herniates through the foramen magnum","Fourth ventricle outflow blocked; hydrocephalus develops"],"caption":"CSF lost through the open defect cannot distend the developing posterior fossa."},{"type":"p","text":"Dandy-Walker malformation is the opposite in fossa size. The vermis is absent or hypoplastic, especially its inferior part, the fourth ventricle is dilated into a large cyst filling an enlarged posterior fossa, and the tentorium and confluence of sinuses are displaced upward. It is thought to arise from abnormal development of the roof of the rhombencephalon, with delayed or failed opening of the fourth ventricle outflow. Most patients develop hydrocephalus and present in infancy with an enlarging head and prominent occiput; ataxia and developmental delay vary, and agenesis of the corpus callosum often coexists."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An adult has wasting of the hand muscles and lost pain and temperature over both shoulders, with normal vibration sense. Which malformation, and which fibers fail first?","explain":"Chiari I with a cervical syrinx. The cavity first cuts spinothalamic fibers crossing in the anterior white commissure, sparing the dorsal columns, then reaches the anterior horns.","check":"The Chiari I and syrinx paragraphs."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Neural tube development links biochemistry, signaling, and cell biology. Folate one-carbon chemistry supplies thymidylate and purines for the dividing neural folds, so maternal folate status at conception, MTHFR variants, and drugs that interfere with folate, such as valproate, alter the risk of open defects. SHH, which depends on cholesterol, divides the forebrain and the face together, which is why trisomy 13, Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome, and Smoothened blockade all produce holoprosencephaly with midline facial defects. Cell origin predicts where disease appears: crest tumors and migration failures (neuroblastoma, pheochromocytoma, melanoma, Hirschsprung disease) arise outside the CNS, oligodendrocyte disease spares peripheral nerve, Schwann cell disease spares the brain, and mesodermal microglia are the CNS cells HIV infects."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and pathology connect these steps to findings. The ventricles are the persistent lumen of the tube, so hydrocephalus is localized by which ventricles dilate, and the posterior fossa malformations are separated by fossa size and CSF flow. There are limits: MSAFP is a screening probability affected by dating, twins, and abdominal wall defects; the hydrodynamic explanations for Chiari II and for syrinx formation are well supported but not proven; and thresholds such as the 5 mm tonsillar descent are imaging conventions rather than sharp biological boundaries."}],"summary":["Notochord and prechordal mesoderm block BMP4 with noggin, chordin, and follistatin, so the overlying ectoderm becomes the neural plate in week 3.","Neural folds fuse first in the future cervical region and close toward both ends; the cranial neuropore closes about day 25 and the caudal about day 28.","Three primary vesicles become five secondary vesicles, and each lumen persists as a ventricle: lateral (telencephalon), third (diencephalon), aqueduct (mesencephalon), fourth (rhombencephalon).","Retina, optic nerve, posterior pituitary, and pineal are diencephalic outgrowths; the anterior pituitary is oral ectoderm.","Neuroectoderm forms CNS neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and ependyma; neural crest forms peripheral ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, and melanocytes; microglia are mesodermal.","Open defects (myelomeningocele, anencephaly) raise amniotic and maternal AFP and amniotic acetylcholinesterase; skin-covered defects do not.","Folate supplies one-carbon units for thymidylate and purine synthesis in the rapidly dividing neural folds, so maternal folate status at conception sets risk.","Holoprosencephaly is failed forebrain division from defective SHH midline signaling (SHH mutation, trisomy 13, cholesterol synthesis defects), and the face parallels the brain.","Chiari I is tonsillar descent with a small fossa and syringomyelia; Chiari II is vermis and medulla descent with myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus.","Dandy-Walker is vermis agenesis with a cystic fourth ventricle in an enlarged posterior fossa, usually with hydrocephalus."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Central Nervous System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, malformations and developmental diseases"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Nerve Tissue and the Nervous System"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Folic acid and one-carbon metabolism; nucleotide synthesis"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back, vertebral column and spinal cord; Head, cranial cavity"}]},"NE-2":{"unit":"NE-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01","title":"Neuron and Nerve Histology","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the microscopic organization of nervous tissue: the parts and shapes of neurons, the connective tissue wrappings of a peripheral nerve, the glial cells that myelinate, support, defend, and line the nervous system, and the way neurons and axons respond to injury. Board questions usually show a cell or a lesion and ask you to name the cell involved, the disease that targets it, or what happens after an axon is cut or a neuron loses its oxygen supply. Knowing why the axon hillock lacks Nissl substance, why one oligodendrocyte matters more than one Schwann cell, and why peripheral axons regrow answers most questions here.","prerequisites":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum, ribosomes, and the cytoskeleton (microtubules, motor proteins)","Resting membrane potential and the Na+/K+-ATPase (IN-3)","Formation of the neural tube and neural crest (NE-1)","Basic tissue types: epithelium, loose and dense connective tissue, basal lamina","Reversible versus irreversible cell injury and patterns of necrosis (IN-8)"],"connections":[{"to":"Neural tube embryology (NE-1)","how":"Neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and ependyma come from the neural tube, while Schwann cells and sensory ganglion neurons come from neural crest."},{"to":"Synaptic transmission and action potentials (NE-13)","how":"Myelin, nodes of Ranvier, and the axon initial segment explain where action potentials start and how fast they travel."},{"to":"Degenerative and demyelination diseases (NE-20-B)","how":"Multiple sclerosis and the leukodystrophies are diseases of oligodendrocytes and CNS myelin described here at the cell level."},{"to":"Neurological vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Red neurons, liquefactive necrosis, and gliosis are the tissue stages of ischemic stroke and global hypoxia."},{"to":"PNS trauma and compression (NE-20-I)","how":"Wallerian degeneration, Schwann cell tubes, and the nerve sheaths decide whether a crushed or cut nerve recovers."}],"terms":[{"term":"Nissl substance","def":"Clumps of rough endoplasmic reticulum and free ribosomes in the neuronal soma and proximal dendrites that stain blue with basic dyes."},{"term":"Axon initial segment","def":"The first part of the axon beyond the hillock, packed with voltage-gated Na+ channels, where action potentials normally begin."},{"term":"Pseudounipolar neuron","def":"A sensory neuron with one process that divides into a peripheral and a central branch, found in dorsal root and cranial sensory ganglia."},{"term":"Perineurium","def":"Layers of flattened epithelioid cells joined by tight junctions that surround each fascicle and form the main diffusion barrier of a nerve."},{"term":"Internode","def":"The myelinated segment of an axon between two nodes of Ranvier."},{"term":"Saltatory conduction","def":"Propagation of an action potential from node to node along a myelinated axon."},{"term":"Reactive gliosis","def":"Hypertrophy and proliferation of astrocytes with increased GFAP after CNS injury; the CNS equivalent of a scar."},{"term":"Microglia","def":"Resident phagocytic and antigen-presenting cells of the CNS, of myeloid rather than neuroectodermal origin."},{"term":"Wallerian degeneration","def":"Breakdown of the axon and its myelin distal to a transection, followed by clearance of the debris."},{"term":"Central chromatolysis","def":"Reaction of the cell body to axonal injury: swelling, dispersal of Nissl substance, and displacement of the nucleus to the periphery."},{"term":"Bands of Büngner","def":"Columns of proliferating Schwann cells inside the old basal lamina tubes that guide regenerating peripheral axons."},{"term":"Red neuron","def":"A shrunken neuron with intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm and a pyknotic nucleus, the light-microscopic sign of irreversible hypoxic or ischemic injury."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C01","obj":"NE-2.1","title":"Parts of a neuron and the three neuron shapes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A neuron is built to receive signals, decide whether to fire, and send the result over a long distance. The large lower motor neuron of the spinal cord anterior horn shows every part clearly, so it is the standard example. Its soma (cell body) contains a large, pale, euchromatic nucleus with a prominent nucleolus, a sign of very active protein synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"The soma cytoplasm is filled with Nissl substance, which is stacked rough endoplasmic reticulum and free polyribosomes. Ribosomal RNA binds basic dyes such as cresyl violet, so Nissl substance appears as dark blue clumps. It extends into the proximal dendrites but is absent from the axon hillock and the axon, so the hillock looks pale on a Nissl stain. This tells you the axon makes little protein of its own; it depends on axonal transport, with kinesin carrying cargo toward the terminal (anterograde) and dynein carrying cargo back to the soma (retrograde). Retrograde transport is also the route by which tetanus toxin, rabies virus, and herpes simplex virus reach the cell body."},{"type":"table","title":"Parts of a multipolar motor neuron","cols":["Part","Structure","Function"],"rows":[["Soma","Nucleus with prominent nucleolus; Nissl substance; Golgi; lipofuscin with age","Makes nearly all neuronal proteins; integrates input"],["Dendrites","Many tapering branches; contain Nissl substance; may bear spines","Receive synaptic input"],["Axon hillock","Cone where the axon leaves the soma; no Nissl substance","Transition zone to the axon"],["Axon initial segment","Highest density of voltage-gated Na+ channels; lowest threshold","Site where an action potential starts"],["Axon","Single, uniform diameter; microtubules and neurofilaments; no ribosomes","Conducts the action potential; relies on transport"],["Synaptic terminals","Boutons with vesicles and mitochondria","Release neurotransmitter onto the target"]],"caption":"The presence or absence of Nissl substance separates dendrites from the axon on a stained section."},{"type":"p","text":"Synaptic input on dendrites and soma spreads passively, and the neuron fires only if the summed depolarization reaches threshold at the initial segment."},{"type":"h","text":"Neuron shapes"},{"type":"p","text":"Neurons are classified by the number of processes that leave the soma. Multipolar neurons have one axon and many dendrites and make up motor neurons, autonomic ganglion neurons, and most CNS neurons. Pseudounipolar neurons begin as bipolar cells in development; the two processes fuse near the soma, leaving one stem that divides in a T into a peripheral branch running to a receptor and a central branch entering the spinal cord or brainstem. Both branches are structurally axons, so the impulse can pass from one to the other without crossing the soma. Their round somas sit in dorsal root ganglia and cranial sensory ganglia, each surrounded by a ring of satellite cells. Bipolar neurons have one dendrite and one axon at opposite poles and are found in special sensory pathways."},{"type":"table","title":"Neuron shapes and where they are found","cols":["Type","Processes","Locations","Note"],"rows":[["Multipolar","One axon, many dendrites","Anterior horn motor neurons, autonomic ganglia, most CNS neurons","Most common type"],["Pseudounipolar","One stem dividing into peripheral and central branches","Dorsal root ganglia, most cranial sensory ganglia","Impulse bypasses the soma; mesencephalic nucleus of CN V is a CNS exception"],["Bipolar","One dendrite, one axon","Retinal bipolar cells, olfactory receptor neurons, spiral and vestibular ganglia","Olfactory neurons are replaced from basal stem cells throughout life"]],"caption":"Shape predicts function: many inputs for integration, a direct path for sensation, a simple relay for special senses."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A section stained with cresyl violet shows a large neuron with one pale cone-shaped region leaving the soma. Which part is it, and why is it pale?","explain":"It is the axon hillock. It lacks rough endoplasmic reticulum and ribosomes, so there is no RNA to take up the basic dye, whereas the soma and dendrites stain blue.","check":"The paragraph on Nissl substance and the parts table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C02","obj":"NE-2.2","title":"Connective tissue layers of a peripheral nerve and the blood-nerve barrier","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A peripheral nerve is a bundle of axons, each with its Schwann cells, organized by three connective tissue sheaths. Reading from inside out, the endoneurium surrounds each nerve fiber, the perineurium surrounds each fascicle (a bundle of fibers), and the epineurium surrounds the whole nerve."},{"type":"table","title":"The three sheaths of a peripheral nerve","cols":["Layer","Surrounds","Structure","Main role"],"rows":[["Endoneurium","Each nerve fiber (axon plus Schwann cell)","Loose connective tissue with thin collagen and reticular fibers, fibroblasts, capillaries; lies outside the Schwann cell basal lamina","Supports fibers; holds endoneurial fluid and capillaries"],["Perineurium","Each fascicle","Several concentric layers of flattened epithelioid cells, each layer with basal lamina and tight junctions","Diffusion barrier; part of the blood-nerve barrier"],["Epineurium","Whole nerve and spaces between fascicles","Dense irregular connective tissue with type I collagen and fat","Protects against stretch and compression; carries the vasa nervorum"]],"caption":"Endo, peri, epi: fiber, fascicle, whole nerve."},{"type":"p","text":"The perineurium is the most specialized layer. Its cells behave like an epithelium: they rest on basal laminae and are sealed to one another by tight junctions, so substances in the surrounding tissue cannot diffuse freely into the fascicle. This keeps the endoneurial fluid around the axons stable in ionic composition and protects axons from toxins, drugs, and infectious agents. The perineurium also gives the fascicle much of its tensile strength."},{"type":"p","text":"The blood-nerve barrier has two parts. The perineurium blocks entry from the surrounding tissue, and the endoneurial capillaries, which are continuous (nonfenestrated) and joined by tight junctions, block entry from the blood. Together they are the peripheral counterpart of the blood-brain barrier. The barrier is weaker in the dorsal root ganglia and at the nerve roots, where capillaries are more permeable. This helps explain why some neurotoxins and immune attacks concentrate in sensory ganglia and roots, as in the root inflammation of Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"At the spinal and cranial roots the sheaths are continuous with the meninges: the epineurium with the dura mater and the perineurium with the arachnoid. The epineurial vasa nervorum feed the nerve, so diseases of small vessels, such as diabetic microangiopathy and vasculitis, cause ischemic damage to nerves."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the sheaths matter after injury"},{"type":"p","text":"The layers that survive an injury decide how well the nerve recovers. If the endoneurial tubes and Schwann cell basal laminae stay intact, regenerating axons are guided back to their original targets. If the perineurium and epineurium are cut through, axons sprout into scar tissue and may form a painful neuroma or reach the wrong target."},{"type":"table","title":"Seddon classification of nerve injury","cols":["Grade","What is damaged","Wallerian degeneration","Recovery"],"rows":[["Neurapraxia","Focal conduction block, often from segmental demyelination; axon and sheaths intact","No","Complete, usually within weeks once myelin is repaired"],["Axonotmesis","Axon cut; endoneurial tubes and outer sheaths intact (typical crush)","Yes","Good; axons regrow along their own tubes"],["Neurotmesis","Axon and all sheaths divided","Yes","Poor; without guidance tubes, axons form a neuroma or reach the wrong target"]],"caption":"The more connective tissue that survives, the better the guidance for regrowth."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C03","obj":"NE-2.3","title":"Schwann cells, oligodendrocytes, and diseases of myelin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myelin is glial plasma membrane wrapped many times around an axon and compacted, about 70 to 80% lipid (cholesterol, galactocerebroside, sphingomyelin). Two cell types make it, and the difference between them explains the pattern of each demyelinating disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Schwann cells versus oligodendrocytes","cols":["Feature","Schwann cell","Oligodendrocyte"],"rows":[["Location","PNS","CNS"],["Embryonic origin","Neural crest","Neuroectoderm of the neural tube"],["Myelin per cell","One internode of one axon","Internodes on many axons, up to about 50"],["Unmyelinated axons","One nonmyelinating cell wraps several small axons (C fibers)","Not ensheathed"],["Basal lamina and outer cytoplasm","Present (neurilemma)","Absent"],["Major myelin proteins","P0, PMP22, myelin basic protein","Proteolipid protein, myelin basic protein, MOG"],["Disease targets","Guillain-Barré syndrome, CMT1A (PMP22 duplication)","Multiple sclerosis, PML, leukodystrophies"]],"caption":"One-to-one in the PNS, one-to-many in the CNS."},{"type":"p","text":"Myelin speeds conduction by acting as electrical insulation. It raises the resistance across the internodal membrane and lowers its capacitance, so less current leaks out and less charge is spent changing membrane voltage. Current therefore spreads passively and quickly along the internode to the next node of Ranvier, where voltage-gated Na+ channels are clustered at very high density."},{"type":"flow","title":"Saltatory conduction","steps":["Action potential opens dense Na+ channels at a node","Inward current spreads rapidly along the insulated internode","Next node depolarizes to threshold and fires","Impulse jumps node to node at high velocity"],"caption":"Only the nodes regenerate the action potential; the internode conducts passively."},{"type":"p","text":"When myelin is lost, current leaks across the bare internode, whose membrane has few Na+ channels, so the next node is reached slowly (slowing) or not at all (conduction block). Block causes weakness and sensory loss; uneven slowing desynchronizes the volley and abolishes tendon reflexes. Demyelinated axons are also heat sensitive because Na+ channels inactivate faster at higher temperature, so MS symptoms worsen with a hot bath or fever (Uhthoff phenomenon)."},{"type":"p","text":"Guillain-Barré syndrome, in its classic demyelinating form, is an acute immune attack on Schwann cell myelin, often 1 to 3 weeks after Campylobacter jejuni enteritis or a viral illness. The mechanism is molecular mimicry (microbial antigens resembling nerve components), and macrophages strip myelin from internodes. Root inflammation raises CSF protein with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation). The result is ascending symmetric weakness with areflexia, sparing the CNS."},{"type":"p","text":"Multiple sclerosis is a T cell mediated autoimmune attack on CNS myelin and oligodendrocytes, producing plaques in periventricular white matter, optic nerves, brainstem, and spinal cord, with oligoclonal IgG bands in the CSF. Because one oligodendrocyte supports many axons, each lost cell demyelinates many fibers, and remyelination is incomplete. Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) is lytic JC polyomavirus infection of oligodendrocytes when cellular immunity fails (advanced HIV, transplant immunosuppression, or natalizumab, which blocks lymphocyte entry into the CNS). Infected oligodendrocytes have enlarged nuclei with ground-glass inclusions, and multifocal white matter lesions follow."},{"type":"example","title":"Demyelinating versus axonal nerve injury on nerve conduction studies","text":"Two patients have leg weakness. In patient A, motor conduction velocity is 25 m/s (normal is roughly 40 m/s or more), distal latency is prolonged, and the response is much smaller with proximal than with distal stimulation. In patient B, velocity is 42 m/s but the response is small. Velocity depends on myelin, so marked slowing with focal block means damaged internodes: patient A has a demyelinating neuropathy such as classic Guillain-Barré syndrome. Amplitude reflects how many axons conduct, so a small response at near-normal speed means axon loss with intact myelin on the survivors: patient B has an axonal neuropathy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a small MS plaque often cause more deficit than loss of a similar number of Schwann cells?","explain":"One oligodendrocyte myelinates internodes on many axons, so its loss demyelinates many fibers, and CNS remyelination is limited. Each Schwann cell serves one internode, and surviving Schwann cells can remyelinate.","check":"The comparison table and the multiple sclerosis paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C04","obj":"NE-2.4","title":"Astrocytes, microglia, and ependymal cells","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Astrocytes are the most numerous glial cells. They arise from neuroectoderm and contain glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP), an intermediate filament used to identify them and their tumors. Protoplasmic astrocytes lie in gray matter and fibrous astrocytes in white matter. Their processes contact synapses, nodes of Ranvier, and capillaries, and neighboring astrocytes are linked by gap junctions into a network."},{"type":"p","text":"The blood-brain barrier itself is formed by brain capillary endothelium: continuous, nonfenestrated cells joined by tight junctions, with few transport vesicles. Astrocyte end-feet cover nearly the whole capillary surface and, with pericytes, induce and maintain these endothelial properties. The end-feet carry aquaporin-4 water channels, the target of the antibody in neuromyelitis optica, which links an astrocyte protein to a demyelinating disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Astrocytes also control the fluid around neurons. Each action potential releases K+ into the small extracellular space, and if K+ accumulated it would depolarize nearby neurons. Astrocytes take up K+ through inward rectifier channels (Kir4.1), pass it through gap junctions, and release it where concentration is lower, often near capillaries; this is spatial buffering. They also remove synaptic glutamate with high-affinity transporters, convert it to glutamine with glutamine synthetase, and return glutamine to neurons. Because this enzyme is also the brain's main way to detoxify ammonia, hyperammonemia in liver failure loads astrocytes with glutamine, causing swelling and Alzheimer type II astrocytes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Spatial buffering of potassium","steps":["Repeated firing raises extracellular K+ near active neurons","Astrocytes take up K+ through Kir4.1 channels","K+ moves through gap junctions across the astrocyte network","K+ is released at end-feet near capillaries","Extracellular K+ falls; neuronal excitability returns to normal"],"caption":"Failure of this system lowers seizure threshold."},{"type":"p","text":"After injury, astrocytes enlarge, proliferate, and increase GFAP, a response called reactive gliosis. Because the brain parenchyma has almost no fibroblasts, the glial scar is the CNS form of scarring. It walls off damaged tissue and restores the barrier, but its proteoglycans also block axonal regrowth (C05)."},{"type":"p","text":"Microglia are the resident macrophages of the CNS. Unlike the other glia, they are of myeloid (mesodermal) origin, derived from yolk sac progenitors that colonize the brain early in development. At rest they have small elongated nuclei and branched processes; when activated they phagocytose debris and present antigen on MHC class II. In HIV encephalitis, the virus enters microglia and perivascular macrophages through CD4 and the CCR5 coreceptor, and infected cells fuse through viral envelope proteins into multinucleated giant cells within microglial nodules. Neurons are not infected; they are injured by cytokines and viral proteins released from these cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Ependymal cells are ciliated cuboidal to columnar epithelial cells that line the ventricles and central canal of the spinal cord. Their cilia help move CSF, and the junctions between them are leaky, so CSF and brain extracellular fluid exchange freely. Over the choroid plexus, modified ependymal cells sealed by tight junctions secrete CSF and form the blood-CSF barrier."},{"type":"table","title":"Glial cells of the CNS","cols":["Cell","Origin","Marker or feature","Key functions","Pathology"],"rows":[["Astrocyte","Neuroectoderm","GFAP","Barrier induction, K+ buffering, glutamate uptake, scar","Gliosis, astrocytoma, Alzheimer type II cells"],["Oligodendrocyte","Neuroectoderm","Perinuclear halo (fried-egg look) on routine sections","CNS myelin","MS, PML, oligodendroglioma"],["Microglia","Myeloid (yolk sac)","Small, dense, elongated nucleus; CD68 when active","Phagocytosis, antigen presentation","Microglial nodules and giant cells in HIV"],["Ependymal cell","Neuroectoderm","Cilia and microvilli","Lines ventricles, moves CSF; choroid plexus secretes CSF","Ependymoma"]],"caption":"Only microglia are not derived from the neural tube."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C05","obj":"NE-2.5","title":"Axon transection: Wallerian degeneration, chromatolysis, and regeneration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because the axon has no ribosomes, everything it needs comes from the soma by axonal transport. When an axon is cut, the distal segment loses that supply and dies, while the cell body survives and attempts repair."},{"type":"p","text":"Distal to the cut, the axon undergoes Wallerian degeneration. For a few days the distal stump still conducts, then the axon swells and breaks into fragments and its myelin collapses into ovoids. This is an active process: loss of a short-lived survival enzyme (NMNAT2) supplied by the soma triggers a self-destruct program that depletes NAD+. In the PNS, Schwann cells stop making myelin, begin breaking it down, and recruit macrophages, so debris is cleared within weeks. Proximal to the cut, the axon degenerates only back to the nearest node or two."},{"type":"p","text":"In the cell body, central chromatolysis begins within 1 to 2 days and peaks at about 1 to 3 weeks. The soma swells and rounds, Nissl substance disperses from the center toward the periphery, and the nucleus moves to the edge of the cell. The rough endoplasmic reticulum is not lost; it is reorganized as the neuron shifts from making neurotransmitter-related proteins to making structural proteins such as tubulin and actin for regrowth. Chromatolysis is reversible if the axon reconnects; if it does not, the neuron may atrophy or die."},{"type":"flow","title":"Peripheral axon injury and regrowth","steps":["Axon is cut; distal segment loses transport from the soma","Distal axon and myelin degenerate (Wallerian degeneration)","Schwann cells and macrophages clear debris","Cell body shows central chromatolysis and makes growth proteins","Schwann cells proliferate in basal lamina tubes (bands of Büngner)","Growth cones advance along the tubes about 1 mm per day","Axon reaches target; Schwann cells remyelinate it"],"caption":"Regrowth depends on surviving endoneurial tubes and Schwann cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Peripheral axons regrow because the environment supports them. Denervated Schwann cells divide inside the preserved basal lamina tubes, forming bands of Büngner, and secrete neurotrophic factors such as nerve growth factor and adhesive molecules such as laminin. Sprouts from the proximal stump enter these tubes and grow at about 1 mm per day, roughly 2.5 cm (1 inch) per month. Regenerated internodes are shorter and thinner, so conduction velocity usually stays somewhat below normal. Axons that enter the wrong tube reach the wrong target, which explains synkinesis after facial nerve injury."},{"type":"table","title":"Why PNS axons regrow and CNS axons generally do not","cols":["Factor","PNS","CNS"],"rows":[["Myelinating cell response","Schwann cells dedifferentiate, divide, and guide","Oligodendrocytes do not form guidance tubes"],["Basal lamina tubes","Present around each fiber","Absent"],["Debris clearance","Fast (Schwann cells and macrophages)","Slow; myelin debris persists for months"],["Growth inhibitors","Few","Myelin proteins (Nogo-A, MAG, OMgp) and glial scar proteoglycans"],["Usual outcome","Useful regrowth if tubes are aligned","Little long-distance regrowth"]],"caption":"Adult CNS neurons also have less intrinsic capacity to grow, so CNS injuries leave permanent deficits even when cell bodies survive."},{"type":"example","title":"Estimating recovery time after a nerve injury","text":"A crush of the ulnar nerve at the elbow leaves the endoneurial tubes intact (axonotmesis). The first dorsal interosseous lies roughly 30 cm (300 mm) away along the nerve. At about 1 mm per day, regrowth takes about 300 days, roughly 10 months, plus a short delay to cross the injury site. Denervated muscle and motor end plates deteriorate over roughly 12 to 18 months, so the hand muscles are reinnervated close to that limit and recovery is often incomplete. Distance from target, not only severity, predicts outcome."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-02-01-C06","obj":"NE-2.6","title":"Red neurons and acute hypoxic-ischemic neuronal injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neurons are the cells most sensitive to loss of oxygen and glucose. They depend almost entirely on oxidative phosphorylation, store little glycogen, and spend a large share of their ATP running the Na+/K+-ATPase. A few minutes of complete ischemia can start irreversible injury, but the light microscope cannot show that injury until about 12 to 24 hours later, when red neurons appear."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hypoxic-ischemic neuronal injury","steps":["Ischemia stops oxidative phosphorylation; ATP falls","Na+/K+-ATPase fails; neuron depolarizes","Glutamate is released and not taken back up","NMDA receptors open; Ca2+ floods the neuron","Ca2+ activates proteases, phospholipases, and nitric oxide synthase","Mitochondria fail; free radicals form","Red neurons visible about 12 to 24 hours later"],"caption":"Excess glutamate signaling (excitotoxicity) links energy failure to Ca2+ overload."},{"type":"p","text":"A red neuron is shrunken and angular, with intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm, a small dark pyknotic nucleus, loss of the nucleolus, and loss of Nissl substance. Each feature has a cause. Nissl substance is basophilic because of its RNA, and when ribosomes break down the cytoplasm loses its blue tint. Denatured cytoplasmic proteins then bind eosin strongly, so the cell looks bright red. The nucleus condenses (pyknosis) as chromatin collapses, a sign that the cell is dead rather than injured."},{"type":"p","text":"Not all neurons are equally vulnerable. In global hypoxia or hypoperfusion, such as cardiac arrest, the first to die are pyramidal cells of hippocampal sector CA1 (Sommer sector), cerebellar Purkinje cells, and pyramidal neurons in neocortical layers 3, 5, and 6, giving a band-like pattern called pseudolaminar necrosis. These cells receive heavy glutamatergic input and have high metabolic demand. Regional hypoperfusion also targets watershed zones between arterial territories."},{"type":"table","title":"Tissue stages after a brain infarct","cols":["Time","Microscopic finding","Cause"],"rows":[["About 12 to 24 hours","Red neurons","Irreversible neuronal death"],["About 1 to 3 days","Neutrophil infiltration","Acute inflammation at the necrotic tissue"],["About 3 days to weeks","Macrophages laden with lipid (gitter cells)","Phagocytosis of myelin and debris"],["About 1 week onward","Reactive gliosis around the lesion","Astrocyte hypertrophy and proliferation"],["Weeks to months","Cyst walled off by glial scar","Liquefactive necrosis removed"]],"caption":"Times are approximate and vary with lesion size and source."},{"type":"p","text":"The brain undergoes liquefactive necrosis rather than the coagulative necrosis typical of other organs after ischemia, because it is rich in lipid, has little supporting collagen, and is digested by enzymes from microglia and macrophages. Red neurons must also be distinguished from central chromatolysis: chromatolysis is a reversible reaction to axonal injury with a swollen soma and peripheral nucleus, whereas a red neuron is shrunken, eosinophilic, and dead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient dies 6 hours after cardiac arrest with prolonged resuscitation. Would red neurons be expected in the hippocampus at autopsy?","explain":"Probably not. Irreversible injury has begun, but red neurons usually take about 12 to 24 hours to become visible, so neurons can look normal on routine sections at 6 hours.","check":"The opening paragraph and the infarct stage table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and cell biology explain the cell types. Neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and ependyma come from the neural tube; Schwann cells, satellite cells, and sensory ganglion neurons come from neural crest; microglia are myeloid immigrants. Because the axon lacks ribosomes, it depends on transport from the soma, which is why a cut axon degenerates distally, why viruses and toxins can travel retrograde to the cell body, and why distal axons are the first to fail in many metabolic neuropathies."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology, microbiology, and pathology act on specific cells. Schwann cell myelin is the target in Guillain-Barré syndrome, oligodendrocytes in MS and PML, astrocyte aquaporin-4 in neuromyelitis optica, and microglia in HIV encephalitis. Myelin determines conduction velocity, so demyelination shows as slowing and block while axon loss shows as reduced amplitude. Light microscopy lags behind events: red neurons appear hours after irreversible injury, and nerve conduction distal to a cut stays normal for several days, so timing must be considered before interpreting findings."}],"summary":["Nissl substance (rough ER and ribosomes) fills the soma and dendrites but is absent from the axon hillock and axon, which depend on kinesin and dynein transport.","Action potentials begin at the axon initial segment, where voltage-gated Na+ channels are densest.","Multipolar neurons are motor and most CNS neurons; pseudounipolar neurons sit in dorsal root and cranial sensory ganglia; bipolar neurons serve the retina, olfaction, and the spiral and vestibular ganglia.","Endoneurium surrounds each fiber, perineurium each fascicle, and epineurium the whole nerve; perineurial tight junctions and endoneurial capillaries form the blood-nerve barrier.","Schwann cells (neural crest) myelinate one internode of one axon; oligodendrocytes (neural tube) myelinate many axons, so CNS demyelination spreads widely.","Guillain-Barré syndrome attacks Schwann cell myelin; MS attacks CNS myelin; JC virus kills oligodendrocytes in PML.","Astrocytes induce the blood-brain barrier, buffer K+, recycle glutamate, detoxify ammonia, and form the glial scar; microglia are myeloid macrophages that form giant cells in HIV encephalitis; ependyma lines the ventricles.","Wallerian degeneration occurs distal to a cut, central chromatolysis in the cell body; PNS axons regrow about 1 mm per day along bands of Büngner.","CNS axons rarely regrow because of absent guide tubes, inhibitory myelin proteins, slow debris clearance, and the glial scar.","Red neurons (shrunken, eosinophilic, pyknotic) mark irreversible hypoxic injury about 12 to 24 hours after the insult; CA1, Purkinje cells, and cortical layers 3, 5, and 6 die first."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Nerve Tissue and the Nervous System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Central Nervous System, cellular pathology and cerebrovascular disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral nerve injury and demyelinating neuropathies"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials, myelinated nerve fibers and saltatory conduction"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Glial cells and the neuronal microenvironment"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-3":{"unit":"NE-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01","title":"Brain and Spinal Cord Structures","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter maps the major structures of the brain and spinal cord to what they do: the primary and language cortices, the basal ganglia loops, the cerebellum, the thalamic relay nuclei, the levels of the brainstem and spinal cord, and the systems that keep us conscious. Board questions are usually localization puzzles. They describe a deficit and ask for the lesion, or name a lesion and ask for the deficit and its side. If you know what each structure normally contributes, which pathways cross, and where they cross, you can work out the answer instead of memorizing lists.","prerequisites":["Neural tube derivatives and the divisions of the brain (NE-1)","Neurons, glia, and excitatory versus inhibitory synapses (NE-2, NE-13)","Major ascending sensory and descending motor tracts and their decussations (NE-5, NE-7)","G protein signaling through Gs and Gi and cAMP"],"connections":[{"to":"Cerebral blood supply and stroke (NE-10, NE-20-A)","how":"Arterial territories decide which cortical, thalamic, or brainstem structures a stroke removes, so vascular syndromes are tested through the localization taught here."},{"to":"Neurodegenerative disease (NE-20-B)","how":"Parkinson and Huntington disease are explained by the basal ganglia circuit, and their pathology (Lewy bodies, CAG expansion) explains which neurons die."},{"to":"Cranial and peripheral nerves (NE-8, NE-20-I)","how":"The brainstem level of each cranial nerve nucleus and the position of the conus and cauda equina underlie cranial nerve and root syndromes."},{"to":"Raised intracranial pressure and herniation (NE-4, NE-20-C)","how":"Herniation causes coma by compressing the upper brainstem arousal system described in the last section."},{"to":"Higher cortical function (NE-11)","how":"The association cortex builds on the primary areas and language areas introduced here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Somatotopy","def":"The orderly mapping of body regions onto a strip of cortex or a nucleus, so that neighboring body parts are represented by neighboring neurons."},{"term":"Homonymous hemianopia","def":"Loss of the same half of the visual field (right or left) in both eyes, caused by a lesion behind the optic chiasm."},{"term":"Aphasia","def":"An acquired disorder of language (producing or understanding words), as distinct from dysarthria, which is a disorder of articulation."},{"term":"Disinhibition","def":"Excitation of a target produced by inhibiting the neurons that normally inhibit it."},{"term":"Medium spiny neuron","def":"The GABAergic projection neuron of the striatum, which forms both the direct and the indirect basal ganglia pathways."},{"term":"Ataxia","def":"Loss of coordination of voluntary movement, with errors of direction, range, force, and timing, in the absence of weakness."},{"term":"Dysdiadochokinesia","def":"Inability to perform rapidly alternating movements, such as pronation and supination of the hand, smoothly and rhythmically."},{"term":"Intention tremor","def":"Tremor that is absent at rest and grows as a limb approaches a target, typical of cerebellar hemisphere disease."},{"term":"Relay nucleus","def":"A thalamic nucleus that receives one type of input and projects it to one specific cortical area."},{"term":"Conus medullaris","def":"The tapered lower end of the spinal cord, lying near the L1 to L2 vertebral level in adults."},{"term":"Ascending reticular activating system (ARAS)","def":"Neurons of the upper pontine and midbrain tegmentum that project through the thalamus, hypothalamus, and basal forebrain to keep the cortex aroused."},{"term":"Brain death","def":"Irreversible loss of all function of the entire brain, including the brainstem, with coma, absent brainstem reflexes, and apnea."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C01","obj":"NE-3.1","title":"Primary cortical areas, language areas, and the deficits their lesions produce","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each primary cortical area handles one kind of information and is laid out as a map. The primary motor cortex (Brodmann area 4) occupies the precentral gyrus and the front of the paracentral lobule; its layer V pyramidal neurons, including the giant Betz cells, contribute heavily to the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts. The primary somatosensory cortex (areas 3, 1, and 2) lies just behind it in the postcentral gyrus and receives touch, position, pain, and temperature relayed from the opposite side through the thalamus. Both strips follow the same somatotopy: the leg and foot sit on the medial surface, while the trunk, arm, hand, and face run down the lateral convexity, with the hand and face given the most cortex because they need the finest control."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the motor and sensory pathways cross, lesions of either strip give contralateral signs, and the arterial territory predicts which part of the body is affected. Anterior cerebral artery occlusion damages the medial strip and weakens the leg more than the arm; middle cerebral artery occlusion damages the lateral strip and affects the face and arm more than the leg. A motor cortex lesion is an upper motor neuron lesion, with weakness that is flaccid at first and later spastic, hyperreflexia, and an extensor plantar (Babinski) response. Facial weakness spares the forehead, because the upper facial motor neurons receive input from both hemispheres. A somatosensory cortex lesion mainly removes discriminative sensation, such as position sense, two-point discrimination, and stereognosis, while crude pain and temperature awareness is relatively spared because it can reach consciousness at the thalamic level."},{"type":"p","text":"The primary visual cortex lines the calcarine sulcus. Each side receives the opposite visual field from both eyes, so a lesion causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Posterior cerebral artery infarcts often spare central vision, because the occipital pole, where the macula is represented, frequently receives collateral flow from the middle cerebral artery. The primary auditory cortex sits on the transverse temporal gyri. Above the cochlear nuclei auditory pathways project to both sides, so a one-sided lesion barely reduces hearing and mainly impairs sound localization."},{"type":"p","text":"Language is lateralized to the dominant hemisphere, the left in nearly all right-handed and most left-handed people. Broca area assembles the motor program for speech, so its loss leaves comprehension intact but speech effortful, sparse, and telegraphic, and patients are aware of their errors. Wernicke area links speech sounds to meaning, so its loss leaves speech fluent but full of wrong and invented words, comprehension poor, and the patient often unaware. Both impair repetition, because each area is part of the repetition loop that the arcuate fasciculus connects."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary and language cortices","cols":["Area","Location","Lesion result","Usual artery"],"rows":[["Primary motor (area 4)","Precentral gyrus, frontal lobe","Contralateral upper motor neuron weakness; forehead spared","ACA (leg), MCA (face, arm)"],["Primary somatosensory (areas 3, 1, 2)","Postcentral gyrus, parietal lobe","Contralateral loss of position sense, fine touch, stereognosis","ACA (leg), MCA (face, arm)"],["Primary visual (area 17)","Banks of the calcarine sulcus, occipital lobe","Contralateral homonymous hemianopia, often macular sparing","PCA"],["Primary auditory (areas 41, 42)","Transverse temporal gyri","Poor sound localization; deafness only if bilateral","MCA"],["Broca (areas 44, 45)","Dominant inferior frontal gyrus","Nonfluent aphasia, comprehension intact, repetition poor, often right face and arm weakness","MCA superior division"],["Wernicke (posterior area 22)","Dominant posterior superior temporal gyrus","Fluent aphasia, comprehension poor, repetition poor, patient often unaware","MCA inferior division"],["Arcuate fasciculus","White matter linking the two language areas","Conduction aphasia: fluent, comprehension intact, repetition poor","MCA"]],"caption":"Fluency places a lesion in front of or behind the central sulcus; comprehension separates Broca from Wernicke aphasia; repetition is poor in all three."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C02","obj":"NE-3.2","title":"Direct and indirect basal ganglia pathways, Parkinson disease, and Huntington disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The basal ganglia send no fibers to the spinal cord; they adjust movement through a loop that runs from cortex to striatum to globus pallidus to thalamus and back to cortex. The key to the circuit is its output: the internal globus pallidus (GPi), together with the substantia nigra pars reticulata, fires tonically and releases GABA onto the ventral anterior and ventral lateral (VA and VL) thalamus. The motor thalamus is therefore held under a constant brake. Movement is allowed by releasing that brake selectively."},{"type":"p","text":"The striatum (caudate and putamen) receives glutamate from the cortex. Its GABAergic medium spiny neurons form two pathways. Direct pathway neurons carry D1 receptors and project to the GPi. Indirect pathway neurons carry D2 receptors and project to the external globus pallidus (GPe), which normally inhibits the subthalamic nucleus; the subthalamic nucleus in turn excites the GPi with glutamate. Counting inhibitory links gives the net effect: two in the direct route excite the thalamus, three in the indirect route inhibit it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Direct pathway (go)","steps":["Cortex excites D1 striatal neurons (glutamate)","Striatum inhibits GPi (GABA)","GPi releases its inhibition of VA and VL thalamus","Thalamus excites motor cortex","Movement is facilitated"],"caption":"Two inhibitory links in series produce net excitation of the thalamus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Indirect pathway (stop)","steps":["Cortex excites D2 striatal neurons (glutamate)","Striatum inhibits GPe (GABA)","Subthalamic nucleus is released from GPe inhibition","Subthalamic nucleus excites GPi (glutamate)","GPi inhibits VA and VL thalamus more strongly","Movement is suppressed"],"caption":"Three inhibitory links plus one excitatory link produce net inhibition of the thalamus."},{"type":"p","text":"Dopamine from the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) acts on both pathways. Through D1 receptors (Gs) it makes direct pathway neurons more responsive to cortex; through D2 receptors (Gi) it makes indirect pathway neurons less responsive. Both effects favor movement, which is why D2 receptor blockers produce parkinsonism."},{"type":"p","text":"In Parkinson disease, pigmented SNc neurons degenerate, with Lewy bodies containing alpha-synuclein, and signs appear only after most nigrostriatal neurons are lost. The motor thalamus is overinhibited (see the worked example), producing bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor, postural instability, a masked face, and a shuffling gait. Huntington disease is autosomal dominant, caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the HTT gene on chromosome 4, and shows anticipation. Its polyglutamine protein kills striatal medium spiny neurons, and the indirect pathway neurons projecting to the GPe are lost first, so the GPi brake weakens and chorea appears. Caudate atrophy enlarges the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles; later loss of direct pathway neurons adds rigidity, while damage to caudate and prefrontal loops explains the dementia and psychiatric change. A small infarct of the subthalamic nucleus removes its drive to the GPi and produces contralateral hemiballismus."},{"type":"table","title":"Basal ganglia disorders by mechanism","cols":["Disorder","Lesion","Change in GPi output","Movement result"],"rows":[["Parkinson disease","Loss of SNc dopamine neurons","Increased","Too little movement: bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor"],["Huntington disease (early)","Loss of indirect pathway striatal neurons, caudate first","Decreased","Too much movement: chorea"],["Hemiballismus","Contralateral subthalamic nucleus","Decreased","Violent flinging of the contralateral limbs"]],"caption":"Hypokinetic disorders raise GPi output; hyperkinetic disorders lower it."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: predicting the signs of dopamine loss","text":"Direct pathway: D1 excitation falls, striatal inhibition of the GPi falls, and GPi output rises. Indirect pathway: D2 inhibition is removed, these striatal neurons fire more, the GPe is suppressed, and the released subthalamic nucleus drives the GPi harder. Both changes increase GPi inhibition of the VA and VL thalamus, so motor cortex receives less excitation and movements are hard to start and scale (bradykinesia)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Early Huntington disease causes chorea, yet late Huntington disease often causes rigidity and slowness. Why does the movement disorder change?","explain":"The indirect pathway neurons die first, lowering GPi output and releasing the thalamus. Later, direct pathway neurons die too, the go signal fails, and the picture becomes hypokinetic.","check":"The two pathways and the table of disorders by mechanism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C03","obj":"NE-3.3","title":"Cerebellar coordination and vermis versus hemisphere lesions","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cerebellum does not start movements, and losing it does not cause weakness. It compares the movement the cortex intended with the movement that is actually happening and corrects its direction, force, range, and timing. Mossy fibers bring a copy of the motor command from the pontine nuclei through the middle cerebellar peduncle, and feedback from muscles, joints, and the vestibular system through the spinocerebellar tracts and vestibular nuclei. Climbing fibers from the contralateral inferior olive enter through the inferior peduncle and signal errors that drive motor learning."},{"type":"p","text":"All of this converges on Purkinje cells, the only output of the cerebellar cortex. Purkinje cells are GABAergic and inhibit the deep cerebellar nuclei (dentate, emboliform, globose, and fastigial) and the lateral vestibular nucleus. The deep nuclei send the cerebellum's mainly excitatory output, mostly through the superior cerebellar peduncle. The cerebellar cortex therefore works by sculpting the activity of the deep nuclei rather than by driving movement directly."},{"type":"table","title":"Functional zones of the cerebellum","cols":["Zone","Main output","Controls","Lesion signs"],"rows":[["Vestibulocerebellum (flocculonodular lobe)","Vestibular nuclei","Balance and eye movements","Truncal imbalance, nystagmus"],["Vermis (medial spinocerebellum)","Fastigial nucleus to vestibulospinal and reticulospinal systems","Posture and axial muscles of both sides","Truncal ataxia, wide-based gait, both sides affected"],["Paravermis (intermediate zone)","Emboliform and globose nuclei","Ongoing limb movement","Ipsilateral limb ataxia"],["Lateral hemisphere (cerebrocerebellum)","Dentate nucleus to VL thalamus and motor cortex","Planning and timing of skilled limb movement","Ipsilateral dysmetria, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia"]],"caption":"Midline structures control the trunk on both sides; lateral structures control the limbs on the same side."},{"type":"p","text":"The vermis works with the medial descending systems that control axial muscles on both sides of the body. A vermis lesion therefore causes truncal ataxia: an unsteady trunk, a wide-based staggering gait, and difficulty standing or sitting without support, while the arms may test nearly normal. The unsteadiness is present with the eyes open, because it is a failure of coordination rather than of sensory input. Chronic alcohol use degenerates the anterior superior vermis and produces this pattern of gait ataxia with relatively preserved limb coordination."},{"type":"p","text":"Each hemisphere works with the lateral corticospinal system that controls the limbs, so a hemisphere lesion causes ipsilateral limb ataxia. The patient overshoots or undershoots a target (dysmetria), shows a tremor that grows as the finger nears the target (intention tremor, absent at rest, unlike the resting tremor of Parkinson disease), cannot perform rapid alternating movements (dysdiadochokinesia), has reduced tone, and tends to veer or fall toward the side of the lesion. Speech may become slurred and irregular in rhythm (scanning dysarthria). The signs are ipsilateral because the path is crossed twice, as shown below."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why cerebellar hemisphere signs are ipsilateral","steps":["Hemisphere Purkinje cells shape dentate nucleus output","Dentate fibers leave in the superior cerebellar peduncle","First crossing in the midbrain","Contralateral VL thalamus to contralateral motor cortex","Second crossing: corticospinal tract decussates in the medulla","Corrections act on limbs on the side of the lesion"],"caption":"Two decussations cancel, so a right hemisphere lesion disturbs the right limbs."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a cerebellar hemisphere tumor and a patient with dorsal column degeneration both sway when standing. Which one gets markedly worse when the eyes close?","explain":"The patient with dorsal column disease. Vision substitutes for lost proprioception, so removing it unmasks the deficit (a positive Romberg sign). The cerebellar patient is unsteady with eyes open as well, because the problem is the coordination of movement, not the sensory input.","check":"The opening paragraph on the cerebellum as a comparator and the vermis paragraph on truncal ataxia with eyes open."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C04","obj":"NE-3.4","title":"Thalamic relay nuclei, thalamic infarction, and thalamic pain syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The thalamus is the paired gray mass of the diencephalon on either side of the third ventricle. Nearly every pathway that reaches the cerebral cortex synapses here first; smell is the main exception, reaching olfactory cortex directly. The specific relay nuclei each receive one kind of input and project to one cortical area, and that area sends dense fibers back to the same nucleus to regulate the relay. The thalamus is therefore an active gate, not a simple wire, and a lesion of a relay nucleus produces nearly the same deficit as a lesion of its cortical target."},{"type":"table","title":"Thalamic relay nuclei","cols":["Nucleus","Input","Cortical target"],"rows":[["VPL (ventral posterolateral)","Medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract: contralateral body touch, position, pain, temperature","Primary somatosensory cortex"],["VPM (ventral posteromedial)","Trigeminothalamic fibers (contralateral face) and taste from the solitary nucleus","Somatosensory cortex face area; gustatory cortex (insula and frontal operculum)"],["LGN (lateral geniculate)","Optic tract","Primary visual cortex through the optic radiations"],["MGN (medial geniculate)","Inferior colliculus","Primary auditory cortex"],["VL (ventral lateral)","Dentate nucleus of the cerebellum and GPi of the basal ganglia","Primary motor and premotor cortex"]],"caption":"Lateral geniculate for vision and medial geniculate for hearing; VPL for the body and VPM for the face and taste."},{"type":"p","text":"Other nuclei serve memory, emotion, and arousal rather than relay: the anterior nucleus belongs to the limbic circuit, the mediodorsal nucleus connects with prefrontal cortex and is damaged in Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, and the intralaminar nuclei carry arousal signals to the cortex (C06). Projections from VPL and VPM pass through the posterior limb of the internal capsule, which lies immediately lateral to the thalamus."},{"type":"p","text":"The ventral posterior nuclei are supplied mainly by thalamogeniculate branches of the posterior cerebral artery, small penetrating vessels that are prone to lacunar infarction in chronic hypertension. Because VPL and VPM together receive every somatic modality from the opposite side, an infarct confined to them causes contralateral loss of touch, position, pain, and temperature over the face, arm, and leg, without weakness (pure sensory stroke). A larger lesion that reaches the internal capsule adds contralateral hemiparesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Weeks to months after such an infarct, as sensation partly returns, some patients develop thalamic pain syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome): constant burning or aching pain on the numb side, with light touch or cold felt as pain (allodynia) and an exaggerated, delayed response to painful stimuli (hyperpathia). This is central neuropathic pain. It is thought to arise because deafferented thalamic and cortical neurons become hyperexcitable and the inhibitory influence that normal sensory input exerts on pain processing is lost."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C05","obj":"NE-3.5","title":"Brainstem levels of the cranial nerve nuclei and the regions of the spinal cord","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cranial nerve nuclei are arranged roughly in numerical order down the brainstem, so the nerves involved in a lesion give its level. The midbrain holds the oculomotor (III) and Edinger-Westphal nuclei at the level of the superior colliculus and the trochlear (IV) nucleus at the level of the inferior colliculus. The pons holds the trigeminal (V) main sensory and motor nuclei, the abducens (VI) and facial (VII) motor nuclei in its lower part, and the vestibular and cochlear (VIII) nuclei at the pontomedullary junction. The medulla holds the nuclei of the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and hypoglossal (XII) nerves. The trigeminal sensory nuclei, however, extend from the midbrain into the upper cervical cord, where the spinal accessory (XI) nucleus also lies."},{"type":"table","title":"Cranial nerve nuclei by brainstem level","cols":["Level","Nuclei","Localizing clue"],"rows":[["Midbrain","III and Edinger-Westphal (superior colliculus level); IV (inferior colliculus level)","Eye movement and pupil signs"],["Pons","V (main sensory, motor); VI and VII (lower pons); VIII (pontomedullary junction)","Jaw, lateral gaze, facial, and hearing or vestibular signs"],["Medulla","IX, X (nucleus ambiguus, dorsal motor nucleus of X, solitary nucleus); XII","Swallowing, voice, gag, and tongue signs"],["Upper cervical cord","XI (spinal accessory); spinal trigeminal nucleus continues here","Weak shoulder shrug and head turning"]],"caption":"The somatic motor nuclei for the eye and tongue muscles (III, IV, VI, XII) lie near the midline, so medial brainstem lesions tend to involve them."},{"type":"p","text":"Brainstem lesions classically produce crossed signs: an ipsilateral cranial nerve palsy, because cranial motor neurons and their exiting fibers do not cross (the trochlear nerve is the exception, crossing before it exits), with contralateral hemiparesis, because the corticospinal tract has not yet crossed. The cranial nerve gives the level; long tract signs place the lesion inside the brainstem."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing a crossed brainstem lesion","text":"Right upper motor neuron hemiparesis, right loss of position and vibration sense, and a tongue deviating to the left. The tongue points toward the weak side, so left XII is affected, placing the lesion in the medulla. Right hemiparesis means the left pyramid is damaged above its decussation; right proprioceptive loss means the left medial lemniscus, already crossed, is involved. All three lie in the left medial medulla, supplied by the anterior spinal or vertebral artery."},{"type":"p","text":"In the spinal cord, central gray matter is shaped like an H. The dorsal horn receives sensory input, the ventral horn contains alpha and gamma motor neurons, and from T1 to L2 a lateral horn holds sympathetic preganglionic neurons; sacral parasympathetic neurons at S2 to S4 sit in the intermediate gray without forming a distinct horn. The gray matter swells into the cervical (about C4 to T1) and lumbosacral (about L2 to S3) enlargements, which supply the limbs through the brachial and lumbosacral plexuses."},{"type":"table","title":"Spinal cord white columns","cols":["Column","Main tract","Carries","Side of deficit below a cord lesion"],"rows":[["Dorsal","Fasciculus gracilis (legs, medial) and cuneatus (arms, above about T6)","Fine touch, vibration, position sense","Ipsilateral (crosses in the medulla)"],["Lateral","Lateral corticospinal tract","Voluntary motor commands","Ipsilateral (already crossed in the medulla)"],["Anterolateral","Spinothalamic tract","Pain and temperature","Contralateral (crosses within one to two segments)"]],"caption":"Where each tract crosses determines which side loses function below a one-sided cord lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"The vertebral column grows longer than the cord during fetal life and childhood, so the cord ends as the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 vertebral level in adults (lower, near L3, at birth). Below it, lumbar and sacral roots descend in the dural sac as the cauda equina. The dural sac continues to about S2, which is why a needle introduced at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5 reaches cerebrospinal fluid among mobile roots without striking the cord. For the same reason, cord segments lie progressively higher than their numbered vertebrae."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-03-01-C06","obj":"NE-3.6","title":"The ascending reticular activating system, coma, delirium, and brain death","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Consciousness has two components. Arousal, the level of wakefulness, depends on the brainstem; awareness, the content of experience, depends on the cerebral hemispheres. Arousal is maintained by the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS), a group of neurons in the tegmentum of the upper pons and midbrain. They include cholinergic neurons of the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei, noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus, and serotonergic neurons of the raphe nuclei. Their projections reach the cortex through the intralaminar and other thalamic nuclei and, by a second route, through the hypothalamus and basal forebrain, which add histamine, orexin, and acetylcholine."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the brainstem keeps the cortex awake","steps":["Reticular neurons in upper pons and midbrain fire tonically","Acetylcholine, norepinephrine, and serotonin are released rostrally","Thalamic relay and intralaminar nuclei shift to tonic firing","Hypothalamus and basal forebrain add direct cortical activation","Both hemispheres are depolarized and responsive","Arousal is maintained"],"caption":"Coma follows interruption of this system at its brainstem origin or failure of both hemispheres it activates."},{"type":"p","text":"Coma is unarousable unresponsiveness with the eyes closed. It requires one of two things: damage to the ARAS in the upper pons, midbrain, or thalamus, or dysfunction of both hemispheres. Diffuse causes include hypoxia, hypoglycemia, liver or kidney failure, drug intoxication, and seizures. A large lesion of one hemisphere does not cause coma by itself; it does so when swelling shifts the brain and compresses the midbrain, as in transtentorial herniation, often heralded by an ipsilateral dilated pupil from compression of CN III. Lesions below the upper pons spare arousal, which is why a ventral pontine infarct can leave a patient fully awake but unable to move anything except the eyes vertically and the eyelids (locked-in syndrome)."},{"type":"p","text":"Delirium is different. It is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness, often with disorganized thinking and a changing level of alertness, caused by a systemic insult such as infection, medication, alcohol withdrawal, or metabolic derangement. The patient can be roused, the course fluctuates over hours, the EEG shows diffuse slowing, and the state usually clears when the cause is corrected."},{"type":"p","text":"Brain death is the irreversible loss of all function of the entire brain, including the brainstem. It is declared only when the cause is known and confounders such as sedative drugs, hypothermia, and severe metabolic disturbance have been excluded. The findings are coma, absence of every brainstem reflex (pupillary light, corneal, oculocephalic, oculovestibular, gag, and cough), and apnea: no breathing effort even when arterial PCO2 rises to at least about 60 mmHg, a level that would strongly drive an intact medullary respiratory center. Spinal reflexes can persist because the cord is not part of the brain."},{"type":"table","title":"Disorders of consciousness","cols":["State","Arousal","Awareness","Brainstem reflexes and breathing","Usual course"],"rows":[["Delirium","Fluctuates, rousable","Impaired attention","Intact","Usually reversible"],["Coma","Unrousable, eyes closed","Absent","Variable","Recovery, vegetative state, or death"],["Persistent vegetative state","Eyes open, sleep and wake cycles","Absent","Intact, breathes spontaneously","May persist for years"],["Locked-in syndrome","Normal","Normal","Vertical eye movement and blinking preserved","Not a disorder of consciousness"],["Brain death","None","Absent","All absent, apnea","Irreversible"]],"caption":"Arousal tracks the brainstem and awareness tracks the hemispheres."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient after a basilar artery occlusion lies motionless, but blinks on command and looks up when asked. Is this patient in coma?","explain":"No. The infarct lies in the ventral pons, interrupting corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers below the ARAS, so arousal and awareness are intact. This is locked-in syndrome, and it must not be mistaken for coma or brain death.","check":"The location of the ARAS and the table of disorders of consciousness."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Localization is the common thread of this chapter. Anatomy supplies the map: which side a pathway crosses, which artery feeds a region, and where cranial nerve nuclei sit. Physiology explains the deficit: the basal ganglia gate movement through disinhibition, the cerebellum compares intention with performance, the thalamus relays and filters, and the brainstem keeps the cortex aroused. Pathology names the processes that remove each structure: lacunar infarcts in the thalamus and internal capsule, Lewy body degeneration of the nigra, polyglutamine toxicity in the caudate, alcohol injury to the vermis, and herniation that compresses the midbrain."},{"type":"p","text":"The rules have limits. Language dominance is not universal, cortical maps are larger for some body parts in some people, and real infarcts rarely respect a single nucleus, so mixed pictures are common. Reasoning from what each structure contributes and where its pathways cross is more reliable than memorizing syndromes."}],"summary":["Primary motor and sensory strips are somatotopic: leg medial (ACA), face and arm lateral (MCA), with contralateral deficits.","Calcarine cortex lesions cause contralateral homonymous hemianopia, often macular sparing; one-sided auditory cortex lesions barely reduce hearing.","Broca aphasia is nonfluent with comprehension intact; Wernicke aphasia is fluent with comprehension poor; both impair repetition.","The GPi tonically inhibits the motor thalamus; the direct pathway releases this brake and the indirect pathway, through the subthalamic nucleus, tightens it.","Dopamine excites D1 direct neurons and inhibits D2 indirect neurons, so both actions favor movement; losing it in Parkinson disease raises GPi output.","Early Huntington disease removes indirect pathway neurons, lowers GPi output, and causes chorea; a subthalamic infarct causes contralateral hemiballismus.","Vermis lesions cause bilateral truncal ataxia and a wide-based gait; hemisphere lesions cause ipsilateral limb ataxia because of a double decussation.","VPL relays the body, VPM the face and taste, LGN vision, MGN hearing, and VL cerebellar and basal ganglia output; a VPL and VPM infarct gives contralateral sensory loss and may later cause thalamic pain.","Cranial nerve nuclei descend in order (III and IV midbrain, V to VIII pons, IX, X, XII medulla); crossed signs place a lesion in the brainstem; the adult conus ends near L1 to L2.","Coma needs upper brainstem ARAS damage or bilateral hemisphere failure; delirium fluctuates and is reversible; brain death is irreversible loss of all brain and brainstem function with apnea."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contributions of the Cerebellum and Basal Ganglia to Overall Motor Control"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Cortex, Intellectual Functions of the Brain, Learning, and Memory"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Organization of the nervous system and motor control"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back: spinal cord, meninges, and the vertebral levels of the conus medullaris"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, sections on degenerative diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-4":{"unit":"NE-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01","title":"Cerebrospinal Fluid Compartments and Meninges","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the coverings of the brain and spinal cord, the spaces between them, the dural folds that divide the skull into compartments, the circulation of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), the barriers that separate blood from brain and CSF, and the interpretation of a CSF sample. Board questions give a mechanism of injury, a CT shape, a pupil, or a CSF panel and ask you to name the space, the vessel, the compressed structure, or the organism class. Each answer follows from which layer is attached where, which opening lies next to which structure, and what inflammation does to the barriers.","prerequisites":["Development and gross divisions of the brain and the ventricular system","Circle of Willis and the territories of the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries","Course of the oculomotor nerve and the corticospinal tract through the midbrain","Capillary types (continuous, fenestrated, sinusoidal) and Starling forces","Innate and adaptive immune cell recruitment (neutrophils versus lymphocytes)"],"connections":[{"to":"CNS blood supply and vascular disease (NE-10, NE-20-A)","how":"Herniation compresses the anterior and posterior cerebral arteries, and berry aneurysms on the circle of Willis are the usual source of spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage."},{"to":"CNS trauma (NE-20-H)","how":"Epidural and subdural hematomas and the herniation they cause are the main mechanisms of death after head injury."},{"to":"Neurological pathogens and CNS infections (NE-19, NE-20-G)","how":"The organism class in meningitis is predicted from CSF cell type, protein, and glucose before culture results return."},{"to":"Hypothalamus and posterior pituitary (endocrine)","how":"Circumventricular organs that lack a blood-brain barrier sense osmolality and angiotensin II and release ADH and oxytocin into blood."},{"to":"Control of breathing (respiratory)","how":"CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier freely while H+ crosses only slowly, so central chemoreceptors track arterial PCO2."}],"terms":[{"term":"Meninges","def":"The three membranes that cover the brain and spinal cord: dura mater, arachnoid mater, and pia mater."},{"term":"Potential space","def":"A space that exists only when fluid or blood separates two layers that are normally in contact."},{"term":"Dural venous sinus","def":"A venous channel between the periosteal and meningeal layers of the cranial dura that drains blood and CSF from the brain."},{"term":"Bridging vein","def":"A cerebral vein that crosses the subdural space from the cortex to a dural venous sinus."},{"term":"Herniation","def":"Displacement of brain tissue from one intracranial compartment into another through a rigid dural or bony opening."},{"term":"Monro-Kellie doctrine","def":"Because the adult skull is rigid, the volumes of brain, blood, and CSF are fixed in sum, so an increase in one must be offset by a decrease in another."},{"term":"Cistern","def":"A wide region of the subarachnoid space where the arachnoid bridges over a deep surface contour."},{"term":"Arachnoid granulation","def":"A tuft of arachnoid that projects into a dural venous sinus and returns CSF to venous blood."},{"term":"Blood-brain barrier","def":"The diffusion barrier formed by tight junctions between nonfenestrated brain capillary endothelial cells."},{"term":"Circumventricular organ","def":"A small region beside the third or fourth ventricle with fenestrated capillaries and no blood-brain barrier."},{"term":"Xanthochromia","def":"Yellow discoloration of CSF from hemoglobin breakdown products, indicating blood that entered the CSF hours or more before the tap."},{"term":"Albuminocytologic dissociation","def":"Raised CSF protein with a normal or near-normal white cell count."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C01","obj":"NE-4.1","title":"Meninges, meningeal spaces, and intracranial hemorrhage","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The space a bleed occupies follows from which layer is attached to which, and from which vessel breaks. The dura mater is the tough outer layer. In the skull it has an outer periosteal layer, which is the inner periosteum of the bones, and an inner meningeal layer; the two are fused except where they separate to enclose the dural venous sinuses, and the meningeal layer folds inward to form the falx and tentorium (C02). The cranial dura is pain sensitive (mainly trigeminal above the tentorium, upper cervical and vagal below)."},{"type":"p","text":"The arachnoid mater is a thin avascular membrane held against the dura by CSF pressure; its outer arachnoid barrier cells are joined by tight junctions, and fine trabeculae cross from it to the pia. The pia mater is a delicate vascular layer that adheres to the brain and spinal cord and follows every sulcus."},{"type":"p","text":"The epidural space lies between bone and dura. In the skull it is only a potential space, because the periosteal dura is the lining of the bone, so it opens only when blood strips the dura away. The spinal dura has no periosteal layer, so the vertebral canal has a real epidural space containing fat and the internal vertebral venous plexus. The subdural space is also a potential space: dura and arachnoid are joined by a weak layer of dural border cells, and this layer splits when blood collects. The subarachnoid space is a real space between arachnoid and pia that holds CSF and the large arteries and veins of the brain surface."},{"type":"table","title":"Intracranial hemorrhage by space","cols":["Feature","Epidural","Subdural","Subarachnoid"],"rows":[["Location","Between skull and dura","Between dura and arachnoid (dural border cell layer)","Between arachnoid and pia, in CSF"],["Usual source","Middle meningeal artery torn by a fracture at the pterion","Bridging veins torn by acceleration and deceleration","Trauma overall; ruptured berry aneurysm when spontaneous"],["CT shape","Biconvex (lens shaped); does not cross sutures","Crescent; crosses sutures, stops at the falx and tentorium","Blood in the basal cisterns and sulci"],["Tempo","Arterial; expands over hours","Venous; acute, or slowly over days to weeks","Sudden, severe headache at onset"]],"caption":"The vessel sets the tempo; dural attachments set the shape."},{"type":"p","text":"In epidural hematoma, the middle meningeal artery (a branch of the maxillary artery that enters through the foramen spinosum) is torn where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet. Subdural blood is venous and low pressure. Brain atrophy in older adults and in chronic alcohol use stretches the bridging veins, so minor trauma tears them, and shaking injures infants the same way. Spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage puts arterial blood directly into CSF, usually from a saccular aneurysm at a branch point of the circle of Willis, most often near the anterior communicating artery. Blood irritates the meninges, red cells appear in the CSF, and xanthochromia develops over hours as hemoglobin is broken down to bilirubin. Blood around the arteries later triggers vasospasm, most often about 3 to 10 days after the bleed."},{"type":"flow","title":"Epidural hematoma","steps":["Blow to the side of the head fractures the pterion","Fracture tears the middle meningeal artery","Arterial blood strips dura from bone, stopping at sutures","Some patients are lucid while the clot enlarges","Rising pressure drives uncal herniation and rapid decline"],"caption":"Arterial source explains both the lucid interval and the speed of decline."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A crescent-shaped collection over one hemisphere crosses the coronal suture but ends sharply at the midline. Which space is it in?","explain":"Subdural. Blood under the meningeal dura is not held by sutures, which bind only the periosteal dura to bone, but it cannot cross the falx, which is a fold of the meningeal dura itself.","check":"The table of hemorrhage by space and the description of the dural layers."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C02","obj":"NE-4.2","title":"Dural folds, intracranial pressure, and herniation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The meningeal dura folds inward to form rigid partitions. The falx cerebri is a sickle-shaped sheet in the longitudinal fissure between the hemispheres, running from the crista galli to the tentorium; its attached upper border carries the superior sagittal sinus, and its free lower border, which arches above the corpus callosum, carries the inferior sagittal sinus. The tentorium cerebelli roofs the posterior cranial fossa, separating the occipital lobes from the cerebellum and dividing the cranial cavity into supratentorial and infratentorial compartments. Its free anterior edge bounds the tentorial notch, the only opening between the two compartments, through which the midbrain passes."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the adult skull is rigid, brain, blood, and CSF share a fixed volume (the Monro-Kellie doctrine). An expanding mass is first offset by pushing CSF into the spinal subarachnoid space and blood out of the venous sinuses, so intracranial pressure (about 5 to 15 mm Hg in a supine adult) hardly changes. Once these reserves are used, small added volumes raise pressure steeply. Cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, so the rise causes ischemia. Ischemia of the medulla triggers a sympathetic surge that raises blood pressure, the baroreceptor reflex slows the heart, and breathing becomes irregular (the Cushing triad)."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Rising intracranial pressure","steps":["Mass expands after CSF and venous reserves are used","Intracranial pressure rises steeply","Perfusion pressure (MAP minus ICP) falls","Ischemia causes edema, adding volume"],"caption":"Edema from ischemia adds volume and raises pressure further, which is why decline can accelerate."},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure is not shared evenly. Brain moves from the higher-pressure compartment through a rigid opening, and the structures at that opening determine the signs (table below). In uncal herniation the medial temporal lobe slides over the tentorial edge and presses the oculomotor nerve against the edge or the posterior cerebral artery. Its parasympathetic pupillary fibers run on the outside of the nerve, so the ipsilateral pupil dilates and stops reacting before ptosis and a down-and-out eye appear; ischemic third nerve palsy in diabetes damages the core of the nerve and usually spares the pupil. Compression of the ipsilateral cerebral peduncle gives contralateral hemiparesis because the corticospinal tract has not yet crossed, and midbrain compression of the reticular activating system lowers consciousness. Downward stretch can tear paramedian branches of the basilar artery, causing Duret hemorrhages in the midbrain and pons. Removing CSF by lumbar puncture when a mass is present can create the pressure gradient that drives tonsillar herniation."},{"type":"table","title":"Herniation syndromes","cols":["Type","What moves","What is compressed","Result"],"rows":[["Subfalcine","Cingulate gyrus under the free edge of the falx","Anterior cerebral artery","Medial frontal and parietal infarct; contralateral leg weakness"],["Uncal (lateral transtentorial)","Uncus of the medial temporal lobe into the tentorial notch","Ipsilateral CN III, posterior cerebral artery, cerebral peduncle; midbrain","Fixed dilated pupil, contralateral homonymous hemianopia, contralateral hemiparesis, coma"],["Tonsillar","Cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum","Medulla (respiratory and cardiovascular centers)","Apnea, circulatory collapse, death"]],"caption":"Each opening has a fixed neighbor, so the compressed structure predicts the deficit."},{"type":"example","title":"Which side is the lesion?","text":"A man with a right temporal epidural hematoma becomes drowsy. His right pupil is dilated and fixed. The uncus herniates on the side of the mass, so the right CN III is compressed, and the pupil localizes correctly. Weakness should be on the left, from the right cerebral peduncle. If instead the right arm and leg are weak, the shift has pushed the left cerebral peduncle against the opposite tentorial edge (Kernohan notch), damaging left corticospinal fibers above their crossing. Weakness then appears ipsilateral to the mass and falsely localizes. The pupil is the more reliable sign of side."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C03","obj":"NE-4.3","title":"Cisterns, CSF circulation, and the lumbar cistern","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pia follows every contour of the brain, but the arachnoid bridges over deep grooves, leaving wide pools of CSF called cisterns. They hold arteries and cranial nerves, so blood from a ruptured aneurysm collects in them, and they receive CSF as it leaves the fourth ventricle."},{"type":"table","title":"Major subarachnoid cisterns","cols":["Cistern","Location","Key contents or role"],"rows":[["Cerebellomedullary (cisterna magna)","Between the cerebellum and the dorsal medulla","Receives CSF from the median aperture of the fourth ventricle"],["Pontine (prepontine)","Anterior to the pons","Basilar artery"],["Interpeduncular","Between the cerebral peduncles","Oculomotor nerves, basilar bifurcation, posterior circle of Willis"],["Chiasmatic","Around the optic chiasm","Optic chiasm, anterior circle of Willis"],["Quadrigeminal (cistern of the great cerebral vein)","Behind the midbrain, above the cerebellum","Great cerebral vein"],["Lumbar","Conus medullaris (about L1 to L2) to about S2","Cauda equina and filum terminale; site of lumbar puncture"]],"caption":"Cisterns at the base of the brain are where subarachnoid blood is seen on CT."},{"type":"p","text":"Choroid plexus in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles secretes most CSF, about 500 mL per day. Only about 150 mL is present at once, so the volume turns over three to four times daily. The choroid epithelium moves Na+, Cl-, and HCO3- into the ventricles, using a Na+/K+-ATPase on its apical (ventricular) surface and carbonic anhydrase, and water follows through aquaporin-1. Secretion is nearly independent of CSF pressure, so a block anywhere downstream raises pressure upstream."},{"type":"flow","title":"CSF circulation","steps":["Choroid plexus secretes CSF into the lateral ventricles","Interventricular foramina (of Monro) to the third ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle","Median and lateral apertures into the cisterns","Subarachnoid space around brain and spinal cord","Arachnoid granulations into the superior sagittal sinus"],"caption":"Flow is one way, from secretion in the ventricles to absorption into venous blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Arachnoid granulations are tufts of arachnoid that push through the dura into the dural venous sinuses, mainly the superior sagittal sinus. Absorption is bulk flow driven by CSF pressure exceeding venous sinus pressure, and the granulations behave as one-way valves, so blood does not enter CSF when venous pressure rises. Some CSF also drains along nerve sheaths to lymphatics. Obstruction inside the ventricular system, as in aqueductal stenosis, causes noncommunicating hydrocephalus; scarring of the granulations after meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage causes communicating hydrocephalus."},{"type":"p","text":"The vertebral column grows longer than the spinal cord during development, so the adult conus medullaris ends at about L1 to L2 (about L3 at birth). The dural sac and subarachnoid space continue to about S2, forming the lumbar cistern, which contains CSF, the filum terminale, and the floating roots of the cauda equina. A needle at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5 enters the cistern below the cord, and the roots usually slide aside. The line joining the tops of the iliac crests crosses about the L4 spinous process or the L4 to L5 interspace. A midline needle passes skin, subcutaneous tissue, the supraspinous and interspinous ligaments, the ligamentum flavum, the epidural space, the dura, and the arachnoid before CSF flows."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A tumor compresses the cerebral aqueduct. Which ventricles enlarge, and would a lumbar puncture relieve the pressure?","explain":"The lateral and third ventricles enlarge because they are upstream of the block, while the fourth ventricle stays normal. This is noncommunicating hydrocephalus, so the lumbar cistern does not connect with the dilated ventricles, and lowering spinal pressure risks a downward pressure gradient and herniation.","check":"The CSF circulation flow and the paragraph on obstruction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C04","obj":"NE-4.4","title":"Blood-brain barrier, blood-CSF barrier, and circumventricular organs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In most tissues small solutes pass between capillary endothelial cells. Brain capillaries close this route. The blood-brain barrier has three parts: continuous, nonfenestrated endothelial cells sealed by tight junctions (claudin-5 and occludin) with very little vesicular transport; a basement membrane shared with pericytes; and astrocyte foot processes covering nearly the whole capillary surface. The endothelial tight junctions are the actual diffusion barrier. Astrocytes and pericytes induce and maintain these endothelial properties rather than block diffusion themselves."},{"type":"p","text":"With the paracellular route closed, a substance must cross the endothelial cell, so lipid solubility, small size, and lack of charge predict entry. Polar nutrients need carriers, and efflux pumps such as P-glycoprotein return many lipid-soluble drugs to blood. Unconjugated bilirubin that is not bound to albumin is lipid soluble and can enter the neonatal brain, staining the basal ganglia (kernicterus)."},{"type":"table","title":"How substances cross the blood-brain barrier","cols":["Route","Examples","Consequence"],"rows":[["Simple diffusion (lipid soluble)","O2, CO2, ethanol, general anesthetics","Rapid equilibration with blood"],["Carrier mediated","Glucose by GLUT1; large neutral amino acids and L-dopa by LAT1","Saturable; dopamine has no carrier, so only its precursor enters"],["Restricted","H+, HCO3-, proteins, most ions and polar drugs","Central chemoreceptors sense arterial PCO2 rather than acute changes in blood pH"],["Active efflux","P-glycoprotein substrates","Keeps many drugs and toxins out of the brain"]],"caption":"CO2 diffuses in and is hydrated to H+ near the chemoreceptors, while plasma H+ enters only slowly."},{"type":"p","text":"Tumors, abscesses, infection, and infarction open the barrier, so plasma proteins and water leak into brain interstitium (vasogenic edema) and intravenous contrast enhances the lesion. This differs from cytotoxic edema, in which energy failure stops the Na+/K+-ATPase and cells swell behind an intact barrier."},{"type":"p","text":"In the choroid plexus the capillaries are fenestrated and leaky, so the barrier moves to the choroid epithelium, modified ependymal cells joined by tight junctions. This blood-CSF barrier, with the arachnoid barrier cells at the outer surface, separates blood from CSF."},{"type":"p","text":"Circumventricular organs lie beside the third and fourth ventricles and have fenestrated capillaries and no barrier, because their job requires contact with blood: they sense its composition or secrete hormones into it. Tanycyte-like ependymal cells joined by tight junctions seal them off from the rest of the brain and the CSF."},{"type":"table","title":"Circumventricular organs","cols":["Organ","Location","Function"],"rows":[["Area postrema","Floor of the fourth ventricle","Chemoreceptor trigger zone: blood-borne toxins and drugs trigger vomiting"],["Organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis (OVLT)","Anterior wall of the third ventricle","Senses plasma osmolality; drives thirst and ADH release"],["Subfornical organ","Roof of the third ventricle below the fornix","Senses angiotensin II; drives thirst and ADH release"],["Median eminence","Floor of the third ventricle (base of the infundibulum)","Releases hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones into the hypophyseal portal capillaries"],["Posterior pituitary","Neurohypophysis","Releases ADH and oxytocin into systemic blood"],["Pineal gland","Roof of the third ventricle, posteriorly","Secretes melatonin"]],"caption":"Sensory organs (area postrema, OVLT, subfornical organ) sample blood; secretory organs release hormones into it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-04-01-C05","obj":"NE-4.5","title":"Interpreting CSF in meningitis and Guillain-Barré syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each CSF value reflects a process. Opening pressure, measured with the patient lying on the side, is normally about 6 to 20 cm H2O. Normal CSF holds up to about 5 white cells per µL, all mononuclear, and no red cells. Protein is about 15 to 45 mg/dL, under 1% of the plasma level, because the barriers exclude large molecules. Glucose is about 50 to 80 mg/dL, roughly 60% of a simultaneous blood glucose, because it enters only by carrier-mediated GLUT1 transport and is continuously taken up by brain cells; a CSF to blood ratio below about 0.4 is clearly low."},{"type":"p","text":"Meningitis changes these values in three ways. Inflammation opens the barriers, so protein leaks in, and edema with impaired absorption raises pressure. The cell type reflects the immune response: pyogenic bacteria recruit neutrophils, while viral, fungal, and tuberculous infections are dominated by lymphocytes. Glucose falls when transport across the inflamed barrier is impaired and when leukocytes, bacteria, and inflamed brain consume it by glycolysis, which also raises CSF lactate. Viral meningitis recruits lymphocytes without major glucose consumption, so glucose stays normal."},{"type":"table","title":"CSF patterns","cols":["Condition","Opening pressure","Cells per µL (main type)","Protein","Glucose"],"rows":[["Normal","About 6 to 20 cm H2O","0 to 5 (mononuclear)","About 15 to 45 mg/dL","About 50 to 80 mg/dL; ratio about 0.6"],["Bacterial","Raised","Often 1,000 to 5,000 (neutrophils)","High, often 100 to 500 mg/dL","Low; ratio below about 0.4"],["Viral","Normal or mildly raised","About 10 to 300 (lymphocytes)","Normal or mildly raised","Normal"],["Fungal (cryptococcal)","Often very high","About 20 to 500 (lymphocytes); may be few in AIDS","Raised","Low"],["Tuberculous","Raised","About 50 to 500 (lymphocytes)","High, often very high","Low"],["Guillain-Barré syndrome","Normal","Under about 10","Raised","Normal"]],"caption":"Values are typical ranges; individual cases overlap, so the pattern is read as a whole."},{"type":"p","text":"Patterns overlap. Very early viral meningitis can show neutrophils, partially treated bacterial meningitis can look lymphocytic, and some viruses (mumps, lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus) can lower glucose modestly. Cryptococcal meningitis in advanced HIV infection may show few cells because the host cannot mount inflammation, yet pressure is often very high, thought to reflect capsular polysaccharide blocking the arachnoid granulations. Tuberculous meningitis forms a thick, protein-rich basal exudate that can entrap cranial nerves and block CSF flow. Gram stain, culture, cryptococcal antigen, acid-fast stain, and PCR identify the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"Guillain-Barré syndrome is an immune-mediated polyradiculoneuropathy, most often demyelinating. The proximal nerve roots run through the subarachnoid space, and their inflamed blood-nerve barrier leaks protein into CSF, but the inflammation is in the nerve, not the meninges, so few cells enter. The result is albuminocytologic dissociation. Protein may still be normal in the first week, and a count above about 50 cells per µL suggests another cause, such as HIV infection, Lyme disease, or lymphoma."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a lymphocytic panel with low glucose","text":"Over three weeks a man develops headache, fever, and a left CN VI palsy. CSF: opening pressure 28 cm H2O, 220 white cells per µL (85% lymphocytes), protein 240 mg/dL, glucose 30 mg/dL with blood glucose 110 mg/dL. Step 1: the ratio is 30/110, about 0.27, so glucose is consumed or excluded, which rules against viral meningitis. Step 2: lymphocytes rather than neutrophils, plus a subacute course, point away from pyogenic bacteria. Step 3: very high protein and a cranial nerve palsy fit a basal exudate, favoring tuberculous meningitis. Cryptococcal antigen and acid-fast testing separate the two remaining options."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does CSF glucose stay normal in viral meningitis but fall in tuberculous meningitis, when both are lymphocytic?","explain":"Glucose falls when the inflamed barrier limits GLUT1 transport and when organisms, leukocytes, and inflamed tissue consume it. Tuberculous meningitis has an intense granulomatous basal exudate that does both; viral meningitis causes milder inflammation with little glucose use.","check":"The paragraph on the three ways meningitis changes CSF."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology set the rules. The periosteal dura bound to bone at sutures shapes an epidural clot, the meningeal dura folds into the falx and tentorium that channel herniation, and differential growth of the vertebral column leaves a lumbar cistern below the conus where a needle can enter safely. Physiology explains pressure: CSF is secreted at a nearly constant rate and absorbed by pressure-driven bulk flow, so any block or added mass inside a rigid skull raises pressure, lowers perfusion, and produces the Cushing triad. Biochemistry and cell biology explain the barriers, with tight junctions, GLUT1, LAT1, and P-glycoprotein deciding what reaches neurons."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology, immunology, and pathology meet in the CSF panel: the type of organism determines whether neutrophils or lymphocytes are recruited, and the intensity of inflammation determines how far glucose falls and protein rises. CSF values overlap between causes and change with timing and prior antibiotics, and a lumbar puncture in the presence of a mass lesion can itself cause herniation, so the panel is read with the clinical course and imaging."}],"summary":["The epidural space is potential in the skull and real in the spinal canal; the subdural space is a split in the dural border cell layer; the subarachnoid space is real and holds CSF and the major surface vessels.","Epidural hematoma is arterial (middle meningeal artery at the pterion), lens shaped, and stops at sutures; subdural hematoma is venous (bridging veins), crescentic, and crosses sutures but not the falx.","Spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage is usually a ruptured berry aneurysm, often near the anterior communicating artery, with blood and later xanthochromia in the CSF and vasospasm days later.","In a rigid skull, pressure rises steeply once CSF and venous reserves are used; perfusion pressure equals MAP minus ICP, and medullary ischemia produces the Cushing triad.","Subfalcine herniation compresses the anterior cerebral artery; uncal herniation compresses CN III (pupil first), the posterior cerebral artery, and the cerebral peduncle; tonsillar herniation compresses the medulla.","CSF flows from choroid plexus through the ventricles and apertures to the subarachnoid space and returns through arachnoid granulations to the superior sagittal sinus.","The adult cord ends at about L1 to L2 and the dural sac at about S2, so lumbar puncture at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5 enters the lumbar cistern below the cord.","The blood-brain barrier is endothelial tight junctions; the blood-CSF barrier is choroid epithelial tight junctions; circumventricular organs such as the area postrema and posterior pituitary lack a barrier.","Bacterial meningitis gives neutrophils, high protein, and low glucose; viral gives lymphocytes with normal glucose; fungal and tuberculous give lymphocytes with low glucose and high protein.","Guillain-Barré syndrome raises CSF protein without raising cells (albuminocytologic dissociation) because the inflammation is in nerve roots, not the meninges."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, cranial meninges and dural venous sinuses; Back, spinal cord and meninges"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cerebral Blood Flow, Cerebrospinal Fluid, and Brain Metabolism"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Neuronal Microenvironment"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Nerve Tissue and the Nervous System, sections on meninges, the blood-brain barrier, and choroid plexus"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, sections on herniation, trauma, cerebrovascular disease, and infections"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous System Disorders"}]},"NE-5":{"unit":"NE-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01","title":"Sensory Receptors, Pathways, and Reflexes","subject":"Nervous System (NEUR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows somatic sensation from the receptor to the cortex. It covers how receptors transduce touch, vibration, stretch, pain, and temperature, how the dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways carry those signals and where each crosses, and how spinal reflexes use the same afferents. It then explains referred pain, how the dorsal horn and brainstem turn pain up or down, neuropathic pain, and the three common primary headaches. Board questions usually describe a pattern of sensory loss, an abnormal reflex, or a pain distribution and ask you to localize the lesion or name the mechanism. If you know which fibers carry what and where they cross, most of these questions become reasoning rather than recall.","prerequisites":["Resting membrane potential, graded potentials, and action potential generation (NE-13)","Spinal cord cross-sectional anatomy: gray horns, white columns, and the anterior white commissure (NE-3)","Dermatomes and the spinal nerve roots (NE-8)","Thalamic nuclei and the location of the postcentral gyrus (NE-3)","Skeletal muscle structure and the motor unit (MS-10)"],"connections":[{"to":"Brain and spinal cord structures (NE-3)","how":"The position of each tract in the cord and brainstem is what turns a sensory exam into a lesion level."},{"to":"CNS blood supply (NE-10) and vascular disease (NE-20-A)","how":"Medullary, thalamic, and cortical strokes produce sensory losses that follow the pathways in this chapter."},{"to":"Nutritional neurological disease (NE-20-D) and CNS infections (NE-20-G)","how":"B12 deficiency and tabes dorsalis damage the dorsal columns, giving loss of vibration and position sense with a positive Romberg sign."},{"to":"Neurological pathogens (NE-19)","how":"Varicella zoster virus latent in sensory ganglia explains dermatomal shingles and postherpetic neuralgia."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and gastrointestinal systems","how":"Referred pain from the heart, diaphragm, gallbladder, and appendix follows the spinal segments that receive their visceral afferents."}],"terms":[{"term":"Receptor (generator) potential","def":"A graded depolarization of a sensory ending produced by its stimulus that triggers action potentials when it reaches threshold."},{"term":"Adaptation","def":"The decline in a receptor's firing during a constant stimulus; rapid adaptation signals change and slow adaptation signals a steady state."},{"term":"Receptive field","def":"The area of skin or tissue from which a stimulus changes the firing of a given sensory neuron."},{"term":"Intrafusal fiber","def":"A small specialized muscle fiber inside a muscle spindle, innervated by gamma motor neurons, that carries the spindle's sensory endings."},{"term":"Decussation","def":"The crossing of a pathway's axons from one side of the nervous system to the other."},{"term":"Dissociated sensory loss","def":"Loss of pain and temperature with touch, vibration, and position preserved, or the reverse, because the modalities travel in different tracts."},{"term":"Reciprocal inhibition","def":"Inhibition of the antagonist muscle through an interneuron while the agonist is reflexly excited."},{"term":"Referred pain","def":"Pain from a deep or visceral structure felt in a body surface area served by the same spinal segments."},{"term":"Allodynia","def":"Pain produced by a stimulus that is normally not painful, such as light touch."},{"term":"Hyperalgesia","def":"An exaggerated pain response to a stimulus that is normally painful."},{"term":"Neuropathic pain","def":"Pain caused by a lesion or disease of the somatosensory nervous system itself rather than by ongoing tissue injury."},{"term":"Cortical spreading depression","def":"A slowly advancing wave of neuronal and glial depolarization followed by suppressed activity that underlies migraine aura."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C01","obj":"NE-5.1","title":"Sensory receptors, adaptation, and afferent fiber types","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A sensory receptor is a transducer. Its stimulus opens mechanically, thermally, or chemically gated cation channels in the nerve ending (for light touch, mechanically gated channels such as Piezo2), producing a graded receptor potential. When that potential brings the first node of the afferent axon to threshold, action potentials fire at a rate that rises with stimulus strength. The type of stimulus is coded by which receptor and pathway is active (the labeled line principle), intensity by firing rate and the number of receptors recruited, and location by the receptor's receptive field. Small, dense receptive fields in the fingertips give fine two-point discrimination."},{"type":"h","text":"Adaptation"},{"type":"p","text":"Rapidly adapting (phasic) receptors fire at the onset and offset of a stimulus, so they signal change, movement, and vibration. Slowly adapting (tonic) receptors keep firing for as long as the stimulus lasts, so they signal steady pressure, skin stretch, position, and length. The Pacinian corpuscle shows how structure sets adaptation: its terminal is wrapped in concentric fluid-filled lamellae, and during sustained pressure the fluid redistributes so the terminal is no longer deformed, while the terminal itself also accommodates. Only rapid changes reach the nerve, which is why it detects vibration."},{"type":"table","title":"Somatic receptors","cols":["Receptor","Location","Best stimulus","Adaptation","Afferent fiber"],"rows":[["Meissner corpuscle","Dermal papillae of hairless (glabrous) skin","Light touch, low-frequency vibration (flutter)","Rapid","Aβ"],["Pacinian corpuscle","Deep dermis, subcutis, joint capsules","High-frequency vibration (best near 250 Hz), rapid pressure change","Very rapid","Aβ"],["Merkel disc","Basal epidermis","Sustained pressure, texture, edges","Slow","Aβ"],["Ruffini ending","Dermis and joint capsules","Skin stretch, sustained joint position","Slow","Aβ"],["Free nerve ending","Nearly all tissues","Pain, temperature, itch, crude touch","Slow; nociceptors may sensitize","Aδ and C"],["Muscle spindle","Within muscle, parallel to extrafusal fibers","Muscle length and rate of length change","Ia: rate and length; II: length (slow)","Ia and II"],["Golgi tendon organ","Musculotendinous junction, in series","Muscle tension, especially active contraction","Slow","Ib"]],"caption":"Encapsulated skin receptors use Aβ fibers and differ in adaptation and depth."},{"type":"h","text":"Pain and temperature endings"},{"type":"p","text":"Nociceptors and thermoreceptors are free nerve endings. Nociceptors adapt little and can sensitize: bradykinin, prostaglandins, and other inflammatory mediators lower their threshold, producing hyperalgesia around an injury. The heat and capsaicin channel TRPV1 opens above about 43 °C (109 °F), and TRPM8 responds to cooling and menthol. Thinly myelinated Aδ fibers carry fast, sharp, well-localized first pain and cold; unmyelinated C fibers carry slow, burning, poorly localized second pain, warmth, and itch."},{"type":"h","text":"Muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs"},{"type":"p","text":"A spindle contains intrafusal fibers (nuclear bag and nuclear chain) lying in parallel with the working extrafusal fibers. Primary (Ia) endings report length and the velocity of stretch; secondary (group II) endings report mainly steady length. Gamma motor neurons contract the striated poles of the intrafusal fibers, and because alpha and gamma neurons are activated together, the spindle stays taut and sensitive while the muscle shortens. The Golgi tendon organ lies in series, its Ib endings woven between collagen strands at the musculotendinous junction. Tension straightens the strands and compresses the endings, so active contraction excites it strongly, while passive stretch of a relaxed muscle mainly excites the spindle."},{"type":"table","title":"Afferent and efferent fiber classes","cols":["Class","Myelin","Diameter","Velocity","Carries"],"rows":[["Aα (Ia, Ib)","Heavy","About 12 to 20 µm","About 70 to 120 m/s","Spindle primary, tendon organ; alpha motor neurons"],["Aβ (II)","Moderate","About 6 to 12 µm","About 30 to 70 m/s","Touch, vibration, pressure; spindle secondary endings"],["Aγ","Moderate","About 3 to 6 µm","About 15 to 30 m/s","Gamma motor neurons to intrafusal fibers (efferent)"],["Aδ (III)","Thin","About 1 to 5 µm","About 5 to 30 m/s","Fast sharp pain, cold"],["C (IV)","None","About 0.2 to 1.5 µm","About 0.5 to 2 m/s","Slow burning pain, warmth, itch; postganglionic autonomic"]],"caption":"Letter and Roman numeral classes; larger, more myelinated fibers conduct faster."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C02","obj":"NE-5.2","title":"Dorsal column medial lemniscus and spinothalamic pathways","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Somatic sensation from the body reaches the cortex by two three-neuron pathways. In both, the first-order neuron's cell body is in the dorsal root ganglion, the second-order neuron crosses the midline, and the third-order neuron in the ventral posterolateral (VPL) nucleus of the thalamus projects through the posterior limb of the internal capsule to the primary somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus, areas 3, 1, and 2). Sensation from the face uses trigeminal equivalents that relay in the ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus."},{"type":"h","text":"Dorsal column medial lemniscus (DCML)"},{"type":"p","text":"Large Aβ fibers carrying fine touch, vibration, two-point discrimination, and conscious proprioception ascend on the same side without synapsing. Fibers from below about T6 (lower limb and lower trunk) form the medial fasciculus gracilis; fibers from T6 and above (upper limb) add laterally as the fasciculus cuneatus. They synapse in the nuclei gracilis and cuneatus of the caudal medulla, and the second-order axons sweep across as internal arcuate fibers (the sensory decussation) to form the contralateral medial lemniscus."},{"type":"flow","title":"DCML pathway","steps":["Receptor, Aβ fiber, cell body in dorsal root ganglion","Ascends ipsilaterally in fasciculus gracilis or cuneatus","Synapse in nucleus gracilis or cuneatus, caudal medulla","Crosses as internal arcuate fibers","Rises as contralateral medial lemniscus to VPL","Internal capsule to postcentral gyrus"],"caption":"The DCML stays ipsilateral throughout the spinal cord and crosses in the lower medulla."},{"type":"h","text":"Spinothalamic tract (anterolateral system)"},{"type":"p","text":"Aδ and C fibers carrying pain, temperature, and crude touch enter laterally and may run up or down one or two segments in the dorsolateral tract of Lissauer before synapsing in the dorsal horn (laminae I, II, and V). Second-order axons cross within one or two segments in the anterior white commissure and ascend in the contralateral anterolateral white matter. Fibers carrying fast Aδ pain run directly to VPL and allow localization. Slow C fiber pain also projects to the reticular formation, periaqueductal gray, and intralaminar thalamic nuclei, which explains its arousing, unpleasant quality. Because each new segment adds fibers medially, sacral fibers lie most lateral and cervical fibers most medial."},{"type":"flow","title":"Spinothalamic pathway","steps":["Free nerve ending, Aδ or C fiber, dorsal root ganglion","Lissauer tract, synapse in dorsal horn","Crosses in anterior white commissure within 1 to 2 segments","Ascends in contralateral anterolateral column","Synapse in VPL (and reticular, intralaminar targets)","Internal capsule to postcentral gyrus"],"caption":"The spinothalamic tract crosses almost at once, so in the cord it carries information from the opposite side."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting sensory loss from lesion level","cols":["Lesion","Touch, vibration, position","Pain and temperature","Example"],"rows":[["Dorsal root","Ipsilateral, dermatomal","Ipsilateral, dermatomal","Disc herniation compressing a root"],["Both dorsal columns","Lost bilaterally below lesion","Spared","B12 deficiency, tabes dorsalis"],["Cord hemisection","Ipsilateral below lesion","Contralateral, from 1 to 2 segments below","Brown-Séquard (C03)"],["Central cord, crossing fibers","Spared","Bilateral, only at involved segments","Syringomyelia (C03)"],["Lateral medulla","Spared","Contralateral body and ipsilateral face","Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) infarct"],["Medial medulla","Contralateral body","Spared","Medial medullary infarct"],["Pons, thalamus, internal capsule","Contralateral","Contralateral","Lacunar or thalamic stroke"],["Postcentral gyrus","Contralateral; leg if medial, face and arm if lateral","Contralateral, often milder","ACA or MCA territory infarct"]],"caption":"Below the medulla the tracts carry opposite sides; above it both carry the contralateral body."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a crossed sensory loss","text":"A patient has lost pain and temperature on the left face and the right arm, trunk, and leg; touch and vibration are normal. A crossed face and body pattern means the lesion lies where the face fibers have not crossed but the body fibers have: the lower brainstem. Trigeminal pain fibers descend ipsilaterally in the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus in the lateral medulla, beside the already crossed spinothalamic tract carrying the right body. The medial lemniscus lies medially, so it is spared. The lesion is in the left lateral medulla (vertebral or PICA territory)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C03","obj":"NE-5.3","title":"Brown-Séquard hemisection and syringomyelia","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Brown-Séquard syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"A hemisection cuts every tract on one half of the cord, usually from penetrating trauma or a tumor. The lateral corticospinal tract has already crossed at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla, so weakness is ipsilateral, with upper motor neuron signs below the lesion (spasticity, hyperreflexia, an extensor plantar response) once spinal shock passes. The dorsal columns have not yet crossed, so vibration, position, and fine touch are lost ipsilaterally. The spinothalamic tract has already crossed, so pain and temperature are lost contralaterally, starting one or two segments below the lesion, because opposite-side fibers entering just below it rise one or two segments on their own side and cross above the lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"At the level of the lesion itself, the damaged dorsal root entry zone and ventral horn cause a narrow ipsilateral band of loss of all sensation with lower motor neuron signs in that segment. A lesion at or above T1 can also interrupt descending sympathetic fibers on their way to the T1 to T2 preganglionic neurons, adding an ipsilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis)."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in Brown-Séquard syndrome","cols":["Finding","Tract or structure","Side","Extent"],"rows":[["Spastic weakness, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign","Lateral corticospinal (crossed in medulla)","Ipsilateral","Below lesion"],["Loss of vibration, position, fine touch","Dorsal column (crosses in medulla)","Ipsilateral","Below lesion"],["Loss of pain and temperature","Spinothalamic (crossed in cord)","Contralateral","From 1 to 2 segments below lesion"],["Band of total anesthesia, flaccid weakness","Dorsal root entry zone, ventral horn","Ipsilateral","At lesion level"],["Horner syndrome","Descending sympathetic fibers","Ipsilateral","Lesion at or above T1"]],"caption":"Motor and dorsal column signs share a side; pain and temperature loss is opposite."},{"type":"h","text":"Syringomyelia"},{"type":"p","text":"A syrinx is a fluid-filled cavity in the central cord, most often cervical, commonly associated with a Chiari I malformation or following trauma. It first destroys the spinothalamic fibers crossing in the anterior white commissure from both sides. The result is bilateral loss of pain and temperature limited to the segments involved, classically a cape over the shoulders and arms, with touch, vibration, and position preserved because the dorsal columns lie outside the cavity. Below the cavity sensation is normal, since fibers from lower segments crossed lower down. Painless burns on the hands are typical."},{"type":"p","text":"As the cavity enlarges it reaches the ventral horns, causing lower motor neuron signs (wasting of the intrinsic hand muscles, lost arm reflexes), then the lateral corticospinal tracts, causing upper motor neuron signs in the legs. Extension into the lateral horn at T1 can add Horner syndrome, and loss of protective pain sensation can lead to neuropathic (Charcot) joints of the shoulder or elbow."},{"type":"example","title":"Working out a hemisection","text":"A man is stabbed in the back. His right leg is weak with brisk reflexes and an extensor plantar response, and he cannot feel vibration or position in the right foot. Pinprick and temperature are lost on the left from about the umbilicus down, with a narrow band of total loss on the right just above. Ipsilateral weakness and dorsal column loss put the lesion on the right. The contralateral pinprick loss begins near T10, and spinothalamic loss starts one or two segments below the lesion, so the lesion is about T8 to T9, matching the right-sided band. Diagnosis: right hemisection near T9 (Brown-Séquard)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a syrinx spanning C5 to T1 spare pain sensation in the legs, while a hemisection at C6 removes it from the opposite leg?","explain":"The syrinx damages only fibers crossing at C5 to T1; leg fibers crossed in the lumbosacral cord and ascend in the anterolateral columns outside the cavity. A hemisection cuts the ascending tract itself, which carries all fibers from the opposite side below the lesion.","check":"The crossing point of the spinothalamic tract and the somatotopy of the anterolateral column in C02."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C04","obj":"NE-5.4","title":"Spinal reflexes and reflex root levels","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A reflex arc has a receptor, an afferent neuron, one or more central synapses, an efferent neuron, and an effector. Spinal reflexes run without the brain, but descending pathways constantly adjust their gain, which is why reflex testing examines both the arc and its supervision."},{"type":"h","text":"Stretch (myotatic) reflex"},{"type":"p","text":"Tapping a tendon briefly stretches the muscle and its spindles. Ia afferents fire and synapse directly on alpha motor neurons of the same muscle and its synergists, making this the only monosynaptic reflex. Collaterals excite Ia inhibitory interneurons that suppress the antagonist (reciprocal inhibition). The reflex is negative feedback on muscle length: stretch causes contraction that restores length. Gamma motor neuron activity sets spindle sensitivity, so it raises or lowers reflex gain."},{"type":"flow","title":"Monosynaptic stretch reflex","steps":["Tendon tap stretches muscle and spindle","Ia afferent fires, enters dorsal root","Direct synapse on alpha motor neuron of same muscle","Ia inhibitory interneuron relaxes antagonist","Muscle contracts, restoring length (reflex jerk)"],"caption":"One central synapse between afferent and efferent, with a parallel disynaptic path inhibiting the antagonist."},{"type":"h","text":"Golgi tendon organ, withdrawal, and crossed extensor reflexes"},{"type":"p","text":"In the Golgi tendon organ (Ib, autogenic inhibition) reflex, rising tension excites Ib afferents, which act through inhibitory interneurons to reduce firing of the same muscle's alpha motor neurons and excite the antagonist. It regulates force and helps prevent excessive tension. The withdrawal (flexor) reflex is polysynaptic: nociceptor input excites ipsilateral flexors and inhibits extensors, pulling the limb away, and reverberating interneuron circuits keep it going briefly after the stimulus ends. Commissural interneurons simultaneously excite contralateral extensors and inhibit contralateral flexors, so the opposite leg stiffens to bear weight (crossed extensor reflex)."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparison of spinal reflexes","cols":["Reflex","Receptor and afferent","Central pathway","Effect"],"rows":[["Stretch","Spindle, Ia","Monosynaptic to alpha motor neuron","Same muscle contracts; antagonist inhibited"],["Golgi tendon organ","Tendon organ, Ib","Disynaptic through inhibitory interneuron","Same muscle relaxes; antagonist excited"],["Withdrawal","Nociceptors, Aδ and C","Polysynaptic, several segments","Ipsilateral flexion, extensors inhibited"],["Crossed extensor","Same input as withdrawal","Polysynaptic, crosses through commissural interneurons","Contralateral extension"]],"caption":"Only the stretch reflex is monosynaptic; the others use interneurons and spread across segments."},{"type":"table","title":"Deep tendon reflexes tested on examination","cols":["Reflex","Main root","Roots involved","Nerve and muscle"],"rows":[["Biceps","C5","C5 to C6","Musculocutaneous; biceps brachii"],["Brachioradialis","C6","C5 to C6","Radial; brachioradialis"],["Triceps","C7","C7 to C8","Radial; triceps brachii"],["Patellar","L4","L3 to L4","Femoral; quadriceps femoris"],["Achilles","S1","S1 to S2","Tibial; gastrocnemius and soleus"]],"caption":"A root lesion weakens or abolishes the reflex it serves; an S1 radiculopathy removes the ankle jerk while the knee jerk is preserved."},{"type":"h","text":"Reflexes in disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Any break in the arc (peripheral neuropathy, radiculopathy, ventral horn disease, neuromuscular disease) reduces or abolishes the reflex. An upper motor neuron lesion removes descending inhibition of alpha and gamma motor neurons, so after the acute phase of spinal shock, stretch reflexes become brisk, spread to neighboring muscles, and may show clonus, while the polysynaptic plantar reflex reverts to the extensor (Babinski) response."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a C6 cord injury has absent biceps reflexes but brisk patellar and Achilles reflexes. Explain both findings with one lesion.","explain":"At C5 to C6 the injury destroys the reflex arc itself (ventral horn and roots), a lower motor neuron pattern, so the biceps reflex is lost. Below the lesion the lumbar and sacral arcs are intact but have lost descending inhibition, an upper motor neuron pattern, so they are exaggerated.","check":"The reflex root table and the Reflexes in disease paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C05","obj":"NE-5.5","title":"Visceral and referred pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Visceral pain differs from somatic pain. Viscera are sparsely innervated, mostly by C fibers that travel with sympathetic nerves (and, for the pelvic organs, with pelvic splanchnic nerves) to the dorsal root ganglia. Cutting or burning the gut causes little pain, while distension, ischemia, inflammation, and traction on the mesentery are strong stimuli. Because the afferents are few, overlap several segments, and often arrive from both sides, visceral pain is dull, poorly localized, often felt in the midline, and accompanied by autonomic signs such as nausea, sweating, and pallor. When disease spreads to the parietal pleura or peritoneum, which have somatic innervation, pain becomes sharp and well localized."},{"type":"h","text":"Convergence and projection"},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral afferents enter the cord at the segments that supplied the organ during development and synapse on the same dorsal horn projection neurons (many in lamina V) that receive skin afferents from those dermatomes. The brain has learned to read activity in these shared neurons as coming from the skin, which supplies far more input, so it projects visceral pain onto the matching dermatomes. Referral therefore follows spinal segments, not the organ's position in the body."},{"type":"p","text":"The heart develops in the neck and upper thorax, and its afferents return with the cardiac sympathetic nerves to about T1 to T4. Ischemic pain is therefore felt as substernal pressure spreading to the left chest and the medial left arm (T1 to T2 dermatomes). The central diaphragm, along with the mediastinal pleura and pericardium, is sensory-supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5), so blood, pus, or inflammation under the diaphragm or around the heart is felt at the shoulder tip, which the supraclavicular nerves (C3 to C4) supply. The peripheral diaphragm is supplied by lower intercostal nerves and refers to the adjacent chest and abdominal wall."},{"type":"table","title":"Common referral patterns","cols":["Source","Afferent pathway and segments","Where pain is felt"],"rows":[["Heart (ischemia)","Cardiac sympathetic afferents, about T1 to T4","Substernal, left chest, medial left arm; often neck and jaw"],["Central diaphragm, pericardium","Phrenic nerve, C3 to C5","Shoulder tip and lower neck (C3 to C4 dermatomes)"],["Gallbladder","Greater splanchnic, about T6 to T9","Epigastrium and right upper quadrant; right shoulder if diaphragm irritated"],["Appendix (early)","Lesser splanchnic, about T10","Periumbilical, then right lower quadrant when parietal peritoneum is inflamed"]],"caption":"Visceral pain is felt over dermatomes of the segments that receive the organ's afferents; spread to parietal serosa makes it local and sharp."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C06","obj":"NE-5.6","title":"Pain modulation and neuropathic pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pain is not a fixed readout of tissue damage. Its main relay, the dorsal horn, is a site where incoming nociceptive signals are amplified or suppressed by local circuits and by pathways descending from the brainstem."},{"type":"h","text":"Gate control"},{"type":"p","text":"In the gate control model (Melzack and Wall), collaterals of large Aβ touch fibers excite inhibitory interneurons in the substantia gelatinosa (lamina II), which release GABA, glycine, or enkephalin onto spinothalamic projection neurons and reduce their response to Aδ and C input. Activity in small pain fibers inhibits these interneurons and opens the gate. This explains why rubbing an injured area or applying transcutaneous electrical stimulation can reduce pain."},{"type":"h","text":"Descending inhibition and endogenous opioids"},{"type":"p","text":"The periaqueductal gray (PAG) of the midbrain receives input from the cortex, amygdala, and hypothalamus. It excites the rostral ventromedial medulla, including the serotonergic nucleus raphe magnus, and noradrenergic neurons of the pons such as the locus coeruleus. Their axons descend in the dorsolateral funiculus to the dorsal horn, where serotonin and norepinephrine (through alpha-2 receptors) inhibit projection neurons and activate local enkephalin interneurons. This system underlies stress-induced analgesia."},{"type":"p","text":"Endogenous opioids come from three precursors: beta-endorphin from pro-opiomelanocortin (mainly mu receptors), enkephalins from proenkephalin (mainly delta), and dynorphins from prodynorphin (mainly kappa). All three receptors couple to Gi/o proteins. Presynaptically they close voltage-gated Ca2+ channels on nociceptor terminals, reducing release of glutamate and substance P; postsynaptically they open G protein-gated inwardly rectifying K+ channels, hyperpolarizing the projection neuron. In the PAG, opioids inhibit GABAergic interneurons, which disinhibits the descending pathway."},{"type":"flow","title":"Descending pain inhibition","steps":["Cortex, amygdala, hypothalamus activate the PAG","Opioids in PAG remove local GABA inhibition","PAG excites raphe magnus (serotonin) and locus coeruleus (norepinephrine)","Fibers descend in dorsolateral funiculus to dorsal horn","Enkephalin interneurons and monoamines inhibit pain transmission","Less nociceptive signal ascends in spinothalamic tract"],"caption":"Brainstem control of the dorsal horn; opioids act both in the PAG and at the spinal synapse."},{"type":"h","text":"Sensitization and neuropathic pain"},{"type":"p","text":"Repeated C fiber input releases glutamate and substance P; the sustained depolarization removes the Mg2+ block of NMDA receptors on dorsal horn neurons, progressively increasing their output (wind-up) and producing central sensitization, so touch carried by Aβ fibers can be felt as pain (allodynia). Neuropathic pain arises when the somatosensory system itself is damaged. Injured axons accumulate Na+ channels and fire ectopically, dorsal horn inhibitory interneurons are lost, and central maps reorganize, so pain persists without ongoing tissue injury. It is typically burning, shooting, or electric, and often coexists with reduced sensation in the same area."},{"type":"table","title":"Neuropathic pain syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Site of injury","Mechanism","Typical features"],"rows":[["Postherpetic neuralgia","Dorsal root or trigeminal ganglion and nerve","Varicella zoster reactivation damages sensory neurons, leaving ectopic firing and central sensitization","Pain persisting 3 months or more after zoster in the same dermatome; allodynia; risk rises with age"],["Phantom limb pain","Severed peripheral nerves and their central maps","Neuroma discharge, central sensitization, cortical reorganization","Pain felt in the missing limb; stump stimulation may trigger it"],["Thalamic pain (Dejerine-Roussy)","VPL nucleus of the thalamus, usually infarct","Loss of normal thalamic processing with abnormal spontaneous activity","Initial contralateral sensory loss, later burning pain and allodynia on the same side"]],"caption":"All three are pain caused by injury to the nervous system rather than activation of nociceptors by tissue damage."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can thalamic pain be severe in a body region where the patient has lost the ability to feel pinprick?","explain":"The infarct removes the normal relay, so pinprick no longer reaches the cortex, but surviving thalamic and cortical neurons become hyperexcitable and fire spontaneously, generating pain that does not depend on a peripheral stimulus.","check":"The mechanisms of central sensitization and the neuropathic pain table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-NEUR-05-01-C07","obj":"NE-5.7","title":"Migraine, cluster, and tension headache","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Brain parenchyma has no pain receptors. Headache arises from the meninges, dural venous sinuses, and large intracranial and meningeal arteries, together with scalp muscles and other extracranial tissues. Structures above the tentorium are supplied mainly by the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1), so their pain is felt in the front of the head; those in the posterior fossa are supplied mainly by the C2 and C3 nerves, so their pain is felt at the occiput and neck. Trigeminal and C2 to C3 afferents converge in the trigeminocervical complex of the lower brainstem and upper cord."},{"type":"h","text":"Migraine"},{"type":"p","text":"Migraine is a recurrent headache lasting about 4 to 72 hours that is often unilateral and pulsating, moderate to severe, worsened by routine activity, and accompanied by nausea or by photophobia and phonophobia. It is about three times more common in adult women. Trigeminovascular activation releases calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) and substance P from trigeminal fibers around meningeal vessels, causing vasodilation and neurogenic inflammation. Sensitized trigeminal neurons then turn normal arterial pulsation into throbbing pain, and central sensitization can make the scalp painful to touch. About a third of patients have an aura, typically visual (a spreading scintillating scotoma), lasting about 5 to 60 minutes. It reflects cortical spreading depression moving across the cortex at about 3 mm per minute, which is why symptoms march gradually instead of appearing at once. Rare familial hemiplegic migraine is caused by mutations in ion channel and pump genes such as CACNA1A."},{"type":"flow","title":"Migraine mechanism","steps":["Hypothalamic and brainstem changes, with or without cortical spreading depression","Trigeminal afferents around meningeal vessels activated","CGRP and substance P released","Meningeal vasodilation and neurogenic inflammation","Peripheral then central trigeminal sensitization","Throbbing pain, nausea, photophobia, allodynia"],"caption":"Trigeminovascular model of migraine; aura is a cortical event that can precede the pain."},{"type":"h","text":"Cluster and tension headache"},{"type":"p","text":"Cluster headache is a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia: severe, strictly unilateral orbital or temporal pain lasting about 15 to 180 minutes, recurring up to several times a day in bouts of weeks, often at the same time of night, and more common in men. Imaging during attacks shows activation of the posterior hypothalamus, which fits the circadian timing. Trigeminal pain activates a reflex parasympathetic outflow from the superior salivatory nucleus through the facial nerve and pterygopalatine ganglion, producing ipsilateral tearing, conjunctival injection, nasal congestion, and rhinorrhea. Swelling around the internal carotid artery can compress its sympathetic plexus, adding ipsilateral ptosis and miosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Tension-type headache, the most common primary headache, is bilateral, pressing or band-like, mild to moderate, and not worsened by routine activity. Nausea is absent and at most one of photophobia or phonophobia is present. Its mechanism is less defined; tender pericranial muscles and, in the chronic form, central sensitization of pain pathways are thought to contribute."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the primary headaches","cols":["Feature","Migraine","Cluster","Tension-type"],"rows":[["Location","Often unilateral","Strictly unilateral, orbital or temporal","Bilateral, band-like"],["Quality and severity","Pulsating, moderate to severe","Boring, very severe","Pressing, mild to moderate"],["Duration","About 4 to 72 hours","About 15 to 180 minutes, in bouts","About 30 minutes to 7 days"],["Associated features","Nausea, photophobia, phonophobia, aura in about a third","Ipsilateral tearing, rhinorrhea, ptosis, miosis","No nausea; at most one of photophobia or phonophobia"],["Behavior","Lies still in a dark room; worse with activity","Restless, pacing","Continues activities"],["Sex","Women more often","Men more often","Slight female predominance"],["Mechanism","Trigeminovascular activation, CGRP","Hypothalamic activation, trigeminal autonomic reflex","Pericranial muscle tenderness, sensitization"]],"caption":"Duration, laterality, autonomic signs, and behavior during an attack separate the three."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology meet in localization. Receptor type decides fiber size, fiber size decides the tract, and each tract's crossing point decides whether a lesion produces ipsilateral or contralateral loss. The same afferents serve reflexes, so a spindle Ia fiber appears in both the patellar reflex and position sense, and dorsal column and dorsal root diseases (B12 deficiency, tabes dorsalis from Treponema pallidum) cause both sensory ataxia with a positive Romberg sign and reduced reflexes. Embryology explains referred pain: organs keep the segmental innervation of the region where they formed, so the heart refers to T1 to T4 and the diaphragm, which arises in the cervical region, refers to C3 to C5."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry, microbiology, and pathology explain how the system is modulated and injured. Glutamate, substance P, and CGRP carry the pain signal, while GABA, glycine, enkephalins, serotonin, and norepinephrine suppress it, and the same Gi/o signaling explains endogenous and exogenous opioid analgesia. Varicella zoster virus, held latent in sensory ganglia by T cell immunity, reactivates as immunity wanes with age and leaves neuropathic pain behind. Patterns in this chapter are classic forms; real lesions are often incomplete, so a partial hemisection or a syrinx at an early stage may show only some findings."}],"summary":["Receptors transduce stimuli into graded receptor potentials; modality is coded by labeled lines, intensity by firing rate and recruitment.","Meissner and Pacinian corpuscles adapt rapidly and signal change and vibration; Merkel discs and Ruffini endings adapt slowly and signal steady pressure and stretch; all use Aβ fibers.","Aδ fibers carry fast pain and cold; C fibers carry slow burning pain, warmth, and itch.","Spindles lie in parallel and sense length (Ia, II); Golgi tendon organs lie in series and sense tension (Ib).","The DCML crosses in the caudal medulla; the spinothalamic tract crosses within 1 to 2 segments in the anterior white commissure; both relay in VPL to the postcentral gyrus.","Hemisection gives ipsilateral weakness and dorsal column loss with contralateral pain and temperature loss; a syrinx gives bilateral segmental pain and temperature loss with touch preserved.","The stretch reflex is the only monosynaptic reflex; biceps C5, triceps C7, patellar L4, Achilles S1.","Referred pain reflects convergence of visceral and skin afferents on shared dorsal horn neurons: heart to left arm (T1 to T4), diaphragm to shoulder (C3 to C5).","Pain is reduced by Aβ gating and by PAG-driven serotonergic, noradrenergic, and opioid inhibition of the dorsal horn; neuropathic pain arises from injury to the nervous system itself.","Migraine is trigeminovascular and CGRP mediated, with aura from cortical spreading depression; cluster headache adds ipsilateral autonomic signs; tension-type headache is bilateral and band-like."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sensory Receptors, Neuronal Circuits for Processing Information"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic Sensations: I. General Organization, the Tactile and Position Senses"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Somatic Sensations: II. Pain, Headache, and Thermal Sensations"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Sensory transduction and the somatic sensory system"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Central Nervous System, malformations including syringomyelia and Chiari malformation"}]}});
